TP.register({id:"lp8-g8",lp:8,grade:"8",mode:"curriculum",checks:7,nextGrade:"high school",build(E){const F=E.F;const ico=(n,s=72)=>F.icon(n,s);const STORY_TITLE="First Bloom";const STORY=`<p>The rule, as Mr. Okpara explained it, was that first bloom meant the day you could stand at the gate and see white without walking up to the tree.</p>
<p>&ldquo;Not one flower,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;Not a branch. White, from the gate.&rdquo;</p>
<p>Linnea wrote that down, which seemed to annoy him.</p>
<p>&ldquo;You do not write it down. You look at it.&rdquo;</p>
<p>The notebook was a school exercise book with a rubber band around it, and the dates ran back forty-one years in three different pens. April 29. April 26. May 2. April 24. The last fifteen years were in a larger hand, and the last four had a small letter g after the date.</p>
<p>&ldquo;What is the g?&rdquo;</p>
<p>&ldquo;Glasses,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;It means I checked twice.&rdquo;</p>
<p>He had stopped putting the g after it and started leaving the line blank, which is why there was a fourteen-year-old at his kitchen table in March holding his notebook.</p>
<p>&ldquo;You will want to start going down there on the fifteenth,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;Every day. Most of them will be nothing.&rdquo;</p>
<p>&ldquo;What if I get it wrong?&rdquo;</p>
<p>&ldquo;You will get it wrong.&rdquo; He took the rubber band off and put it back on, which he did about every forty seconds. &ldquo;The first three years are wrong. Everybody's are. Then you stop looking for flowers and start looking at the whole tree, and the day arrives and you know it, and after that the dates are worth something.&rdquo;</p>
<p>Linnea went down on the fifteenth and on every day after it. The sixteenth through the twenty-first were nothing. On the twenty-second there was a smear of something at the top that was not quite white and that she could only see because she knew exactly where to look, which she understood, standing there, was the whole point of the rule.</p>
<p>She did not write anything on the twenty-second.</p>
<p>On April the twenty-fourth she stopped at the gate and did not have to decide.</p>
<p>She wrote it in the notebook that evening, under forty-one other lines, in the fourth pen. Then she walked it back up the street and put it in his hands, because he would want to know, and because the one thing he had not said was whether the notebook was his or hers now.</p>`;const story=()=>`<h3 style="margin:0 0 6px;color:var(--sea)">${STORY_TITLE}</h3><div class="passage sm">${STORY}</div>`;const ART_TITLE="What a Bloom Date Is For";const ARTTEXT=`<p>A date written on a kitchen calendar is not science. Forty-one of them in one notebook, taken at one tree by one person using one rule, is close to it.</p>
<p>The study of when recurring natural events happen is called phenology, and it is one of the few sciences whose longest data sets were gathered by people who were not scientists. The record of cherry blossom dates kept at Kyoto reaches back more than 1,200 years, assembled first from court diaries and later from official observation. Nobody started it as a climate record. It became one.</p>
<p>What makes a bloom date useful is that a plant is not reading a calendar. It is adding up heat. Many temperate plants begin to develop once the daily average temperature rises past about 50 degrees Fahrenheit, and what matters after that is the total warmth accumulated, not the number of days that have passed. Two springs that begin on the same first of March can run two weeks apart.</p>
<p>Across many Northern Hemisphere records, spring events have been arriving earlier, by roughly two to three days a decade in recent decades. No single tree shows that. The signal is small, the year-to-year scatter is large, and one late frost can move a date by a week. It appears only when a record is long, or when many records are pooled.</p>
<p>That is where the rule matters more than the observer. A date is a measurement, and a measurement needs a definition that does not drift. A record kept for thirty years to one rule and eleven years to a slightly looser one is not a forty-one year record. It is two short ones laid end to end, and the join is exactly where a trend would appear to begin.</p>`;const artText=()=>`<h3 style="margin:0 0 6px;color:var(--sea)">${ART_TITLE}</h3><div class="passage sm">${ARTTEXT}</div>`;const MEMO_TITLE="Plant the Orchard on the Creek Field";const MEMO_SRC="Memo from Councilor Rhea Sandoval to the Marrow Creek town council. April 2026.";const MEMO=`<p>The two acres of town ground between the mill and the creek should be planted as a community orchard this spring, not left as the wet meadow the conservation committee has proposed. Four reasons follow, and then one point from another town.</p>
<p>First, use. The orchard plan puts 48 fruit trees on ground that currently produces nothing anybody eats. At the yields the extension office uses for planning, 48 mature trees would supply the food pantry with about 9,000 pounds of fruit a year.</p>
<p>Second, cost. The orchard is $31,000 to plant and about $4,200 a year to maintain. The wet meadow restoration is $58,000, because it requires regrading the two acres and three years of invasive removal.</p>
<p>Third, time. An orchard is productive in year four. The meadow's own application says its plant community will take eight to twelve years to establish.</p>
<p>Fourth, opinion. I asked the twenty-six people at the orchard planning meeting whether they would rather have fruit trees or a meadow, and twenty-four said fruit trees.</p>
<p>One more point. Ashby planted a community orchard in 2019, and use of its food pantry dropped the following year, which shows that an orchard feeds people who would otherwise go without.</p>
<p>It is also worth remembering that the mill building, which overlooks this field, is on the national register.</p>`;const memoText=()=>`<article><h3 style="margin:0 0 4px;color:var(--sea)">${MEMO_TITLE}</h3><p class="src" style="margin:0 0 8px">${MEMO_SRC}</p><div class="passage sm">${MEMO}</div></article>`;const SRC_A_CARD="Report: “Forty-One Years of First Bloom at Marrow Creek: Digitizing a Single-Observer Record.” By Dr. Yusuf Adeyemi, plant ecologist, State Phenology Network, 2025.";const SRC_A_TEXT="Across the 41 years, first bloom moved earlier by an average of 2.4 days per decade. The trend is not significant in any ten-year window on its own, and the four years the observer marked with a g are flagged in the data as recorded under a changed procedure.";const SRC_B_CARD="A post in a regional gardening group by a user named creekside_tom. No date.";const SRC_B_TEXT="Spring is definitely like three weeks earlier than when I was a kid. You can just tell. Everything blooms way before May now, nobody needs a notebook to see that.";const SOURCES=()=>`<p class="big" style="font-size:1.55rem">Research question: has first bloom at Marrow Creek moved earlier over the last forty years, and by how much?</p><div style="display:flex;gap:16px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:14px">${srcCard("Source A",SRC_A_CARD,SRC_A_TEXT)}${srcCard("Source B",SRC_B_CARD,SRC_B_TEXT)}</div>`;const checklist=items=>`<ul class="qlist" style="margin-top:14px">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const factBox=(title,items)=>`<div style="border:3px dashed var(--line);border-radius:16px;background:var(--surface);padding:10px 20px;margin:14px 0"><p class="fig-label" style="margin:0 0 6px;text-align:left">${title}</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:1.2em;font-size:1.25rem;line-height:1.5">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul></div>`;const bigTable=(head,rows,cap)=>`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="chart" style="margin:14px auto;font-size:1.3rem">${cap?`<caption style="font-size:1.1rem;padding-bottom:6px">${cap}</caption>`:""}<tr>${head.map(h=>`<th style="text-align:center">${h}</th>`).join("")}</tr>${rows.map(r=>`<tr>${r.map((c,i)=>i?`<td style="text-align:center">${c}</td>`:`<th>${c}</th>`).join("")}</tr>`).join("")}</table></div>`;function srcCard(tag,head,body){return`<article style="flex:1 1 320px;border:2px solid var(--line);border-radius:16px;padding:12px 16px;background:var(--surface)"><h3 style="margin:0 0 4px;color:var(--sea)">${tag}</h3><p style="margin:0 0 8px;font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700">${head}</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:1.25rem;line-height:1.5">${body}</p></article>`}const lead=t=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">${t}</p>`;function grid(o){const m={l:o.yl?78:40,r:30,t:o.title?34:20,b:o.xl?56:32};const W=(o.x1-o.x0)*o.sx,H=(o.y1-o.y0)*o.sy;const X=x=>m.l+(x-o.x0)*o.sx,Y=y=>m.t+(o.y1-y)*o.sy;const gx=o.gx||1,gy=o.gy||1,lx=o.lx||gx,ly=o.ly||gy;let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${W+m.l+m.r} ${H+m.t+m.b}" width="${W+m.l+m.r}" role="img" aria-label="${o.alt}" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">`;if(o.title)s+=`<text x="${m.l+W/2}" y="21" font-size="17" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${o.title}</text>`;s+=`<rect x="${m.l}" y="${m.t}" width="${W}" height="${H}" fill="var(--surface)"/>`;for(let x=o.x0;x<=o.x1+1e-9;x+=gx)s+=`<line x1="${X(x)}" y1="${m.t}" x2="${X(x)}" y2="${m.t+H}" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="1"/>`;for(let y=o.y0;y<=o.y1+1e-9;y+=gy)s+=`<line x1="${m.l}" y1="${Y(y)}" x2="${m.l+W}" y2="${Y(y)}" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="1"/>`;s+=`<rect x="${m.l}" y="${m.t}" width="${W}" height="${H}" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/>`;for(let x=o.x0;x<=o.x1+1e-9;x+=lx)s+=`<text x="${X(x)}" y="${m.t+H+19}" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${+x.toFixed(3)}</text>`;for(let y=o.y0;y<=o.y1+1e-9;y+=ly)s+=`<text x="${m.l-8}" y="${Y(y)}" font-size="13" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" fill="var(--ink)">${+y.toFixed(3)}</text>`;if(o.xl)s+=`<text x="${m.l+W/2}" y="${m.t+H+48}" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${o.xl}</text>`;if(o.yl)s+=`<text transform="translate(21,${m.t+H/2}) rotate(-90)" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${o.yl}</text>`;(o.paths||[]).forEach(p=>{s+=`<polyline points="${p.map(q=>X(q[0])+","+Y(q[1])).join(" ")}" fill="none" stroke="var(--sea)" stroke-width="3.5"/>`});(o.pts||[]).forEach(p=>{s+=`<circle cx="${X(p[0])}" cy="${Y(p[1])}" r="6" fill="var(--coral)" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.4"/>`});return s+"</svg>"}const BLOOM=[[41,30],[42,29],[43,28],[44,26],[45,26],[46,24],[47,23],[48,22],[49,21],[50,20],[51,19],[46,14]];const bloomPlot=()=>grid({alt:"A scatter plot of first bloom day in April against the average March temperature, with twelve points. Eleven of them fall along a band that goes down to the right. One point sits well below that band.",title:"Twelve springs at the mill tree",x0:40,x1:52,y0:10,y1:32,sx:30,sy:11,gx:1,lx:2,gy:2,ly:4,xl:"Average March temperature (°F)",yl:"First bloom, day of April",pts:BLOOM});const pointGraph=()=>grid({alt:"A graph with five plotted points: 1 comma 3, 3 comma 7, 5 comma 6, 5 comma 11, and 8 comma 9.",title:"Graph D",x0:0,x1:9,y0:0,y1:12,sx:34,sy:19,gx:1,lx:1,gy:2,ly:2,xl:"Input",yl:"Output",pts:[[1,3],[3,7],[5,6],[5,11],[8,9]]});const tableA=()=>bigTable(["Days after 15 April","0","7","9","11"],[["Air temperature at 9 a.m. (°F)","38","44","51","47"]],"Table A");const tableB=()=>bigTable(["Tree number","1","2","3","4"],[["First bloom","April 24","April 24","April 26","April 22"]],"Table B");const tableC=()=>bigTable(["Average March temperature","44 °F","46 °F","46 °F","51 °F"],[["First bloom","April 26","April 24","April 14","April 19"]],"Table C");const duckTable=()=>bigTable(["Day","0","2","4","6"],[["Duckweed cover (square cm)","3","6","12","24"]],"Duckweed on the creek pool");const ASH_A_CARD="Note from the Ashby town clerk, in reply to a request from Marrow Creek.";const ASH_A_TEXT="Our community orchard was planted in the spring of 2019. Its first harvest of any size was in 2023; before that the trees produced almost nothing. Food pantry visits in Ashby did fall by 18 percent in 2020. The pantry also moved that January, out of the old fire station and into the library annex, which has no parking. Our own year-end report names the move, not the orchard, as the likeliest reason for the drop.";const ASH_B_CARD="From a regional food security brief, Growing Access: Orchards and Pantry Demand, published by the Three Counties Food Council, 2024.";const ASH_B_TEXT="Ashby's pantry use fell 18 percent in the year after its community orchard went in. Ashby is one of several towns in which a new orchard was followed by a measurable drop in pantry demand, and the pattern is strong enough that towns weighing an orchard should count reduced pantry need among its benefits.";const ashbyPair=()=>`<p class="big" style="font-size:1.5rem">The memo says Ashby's orchard shows that an orchard feeds people who would otherwise go without. These two texts both describe Ashby.</p><div style="display:flex;gap:16px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:12px">${srcCard("Text 1",ASH_A_CARD,ASH_A_TEXT)}${srcCard("Text 2",ASH_B_CARD,ASH_B_TEXT)}</div>`;const crewGraph=()=>grid({alt:"A graph of trees planted against hours worked. One straight line rises from the origin, and a plotted point sits on it at 4 hours and 60 trees.",title:"Crew B",x0:0,x1:5,y0:0,y1:80,sx:70,sy:3.4,gx:1,lx:1,gy:10,ly:20,xl:"Hours worked",yl:"Trees planted",paths:[[[0,0],[5,75]]],pts:[[4,60]]});const triGrid=()=>F.coordGrid({max:8,quad4:true,size:420,polys:[[[1,2],[4,2],[4,6]]],points:[[1,2,"A"],[4,2,"B"],[4,6,"C"]]});const STOPS=[{id:1,title:"First Bloom",short:"First Bloom",parts:[{std:["RL.8.1"],dur:140,dir:"Read the story to yourself. Then answer out loud. Read me the exact words that prove each answer.",desc:"citing the strongest evidence for what a story says and for inferences",html:story,qs:["What exactly is the rule for first bloom? Read me the exact words.","Why is a fourteen-year-old holding Mr. Okpara's notebook? Read me the sentence that is the strongest evidence, and say why it is stronger than the others.","Which is the strongest evidence that Linnea has understood the rule rather than only memorized it? Pick A, B, or C and tell me why. A: “Linnea wrote that down.” B: “She did not write anything on the twenty-second.” C: “Linnea went down on the fifteenth and on every day after it.”","The last sentence says “the one thing he had not said was whether the notebook was his or hers now.” What are the two things her walking it up the street could mean, and what is the strongest evidence for each?"],key:["First bloom is the day you can stand at the gate and see white without walking up to the tree. Reads “first bloom meant the day you could stand at the gate and see white without walking up to the tree,” and may add his own version, “Not one flower. Not a branch. White, from the gate.” (RL.8.1, explicit evidence)","Because his eyes can no longer make the judgment the rule requires. Strongest: “He had stopped putting the g after it and started leaving the line blank, which is why there was a fourteen-year-old at his kitchen table in March holding his notebook.” That sentence states the cause outright. The g line, “Glasses. It means I checked twice,” is evidence of the same thing but needs an inference, and “the last fifteen years were in a larger hand” is weaker still. (RL.8.1, strongest evidence)","B. On the twenty-second she saw something, decided it did not meet the rule, and wrote nothing, which is the rule being applied rather than recited. The story makes the point itself: she could only see the smear “because she knew exactly where to look, which she understood, standing there, was the whole point of the rule.” A shows only that she copied it down, and he treats that as the opposite of understanding: “You do not write it down. You look at it.” C shows that she was diligent, not that she judged correctly. (RL.8.1, strongest evidence)","Either that she is handing the record back, or that she is reporting to him as the person who now keeps it. For handing it back: “she walked it back up the street and put it in his hands,” and it is still called “his notebook” earlier in the story. For keeping it: she had already written the date herself, “in the notebook that evening, under forty-one other lines, in the fourth pen,” which is the act of an observer and not of a borrower, and her reason is “because he would want to know,” which is what you tell someone about a record you are keeping. (RL.8.1, two supported readings)"]}]},{id:2,title:"Powers of the Same Base",short:"Exponents",parts:[{std:["8.EE.A.1"],dur:160,dir:"Work on your paper and hold it up. Write each answer as a single power first, then as a number.",desc:"applying the properties of integer exponents to produce equivalent expressions",html:()=>lead("Same base, so the exponents do the work. Multiplying adds them, dividing subtracts them, and a power of a power multiplies them."),qs:["Write 3<sup>4</sup> × 3<sup>2</sup> as a single power, then give its value.","Write 5<sup>7</sup> ÷ 5<sup>4</sup> as a single power, then give its value.","Evaluate 2<sup>−3</sup>.","Write (4<sup>2</sup>)<sup>3</sup> as a single power, then give its value.","What is 7<sup>0</sup>? Use the division rule to explain why.","Write (3<sup>5</sup> × 3<sup>−2</sup>) ÷ 3<sup>4</sup> as a single power, then give its value."],key:["3<sup>6</sup> = 729. Multiplying powers of the same base adds the exponents, so 4 + 2 = 6, and 3<sup>6</sup> = 729. Or: write out four 3s and two 3s and count six. Partial: 3⁸ comes from multiplying the exponents and does not count. (8.EE.A.1)","5<sup>3</sup> = 125. Dividing subtracts the exponents, so 7 − 4 = 3, and 5<sup>3</sup> = 125. Or: cancel four 5s from the factor strings. Partial: the power right with the value wrong is approaching. (8.EE.A.1)","1/8, which is 0.125. A negative exponent means the reciprocal of the positive power, so 2<sup>−3</sup> = 1 ÷ 2<sup>3</sup> = 1/8. Answering −8 treats the sign as if it belonged to the answer. Or: step down the powers, 4, 2, 1, 1/2, 1/4, 1/8. (8.EE.A.1)","4<sup>6</sup> = 4,096. A power of a power multiplies the exponents, so 2 × 3 = 6, and 4<sup>6</sup> = 4,096. Or: write (4²)(4²)(4²) and add the exponents. Partial: 4⁵ comes from adding instead of multiplying and does not count. (8.EE.A.1)","1. Any number divided by itself is 1, so 7<sup>3</sup> ÷ 7<sup>3</sup> = 1. The division rule says the same expression is 7<sup>3 − 3</sup> = 7<sup>0</sup>. The two have to agree, so 7<sup>0</sup> = 1. Or: step down the powers, 343, 49, 7, each step dividing by 7, so after 7 comes 1. (8.EE.A.1)","3<sup>−1</sup> = 1/3. The exponents give 5 + (−2) − 4 = −1, and 3<sup>−1</sup> = 1/3. Or: 243 ÷ 9 ÷ 81 = 1/3 worked out as numbers. Partial: the single power right with no value is approaching. (8.EE.A.1)"]}]},{id:3,title:"White, From the Gate",short:"Word Choice",parts:[{std:["RL.8.4"],dur:135,dir:"Read the story to yourself. Then answer out loud. Read me the words you are talking about.",desc:"the meaning of words and phrases, including connotation, figurative language, and the effect of specific word choices",html:story,qs:["The rule says “white, from the gate.” Compare that with “the first flower.” What does the rule's wording carry that the other does not, and why would a record need it?","Mr. Okpara answers “What is the g?” with two words: “Glasses. It means I checked twice.” What does that answer carry about him, and what does the g really record?","On the twenty-second Linnea sees “a smear of something at the top that was not quite white.” Why is smear the right word there, and what would bloom or blossom have done to the sentence?","She writes the date “under forty-one other lines, in the fourth pen.” What does the phrase “the fourth pen” mean here, and what does it do that “with her own pen” would not?","He says, “Then you stop looking for flowers and start looking at the whole tree.” Explain the comparison he is making about how to see, and how the last two paragraphs show it."],key:["“White, from the gate” names a fixed place to stand and a threshold the whole tree has to cross, so two people in two different years can apply it the same way. “The first flower” names a single object and would send the observer walking around the tree hunting for it, which makes the date depend on how hard somebody looked. A record needs the wording that does not drift. (RL.8.4, the effect of a specific word choice)","It is flat, short, and gives no excuse, which fits a man who says “You do not write it down. You look at it” and will not dress anything up. What the g really records is a change in his method, and under it a growing doubt about his own eyes, which is why the next thing he does is leave the line blank and hand the notebook over. (RL.8.4, connotative meaning)","Smear carries vagueness and almost nothing: something spread thin, not a shape, not a count, not yet a thing. That is exactly what fails the rule, so the word does the judging. Bloom or blossom would have named a flower and settled the question the wrong way, and the sentence after it, “She did not write anything on the twenty-second,” would stop making sense. (RL.8.4, connotation)","It means the fourth observer's pen, counting the three the record was already written in: it places her in a line of people rather than in a moment. “With her own pen” would make it about possession and about her. “In the fourth pen” makes the pen a unit of the record, which is why the last clause can then ask who the notebook now belongs to. (RL.8.4, the effect of word choice on meaning)","He is contrasting two ways of looking: hunting for a particular small thing, which finds something every time and therefore proves nothing, against taking in the whole and letting the change announce itself. The last two paragraphs are the two halves. On the twenty-second she finds the smear only “because she knew exactly where to look,” which is the first kind, and she writes nothing. On the twenty-fourth she “stopped at the gate and did not have to decide,” which is the second. (RL.8.4, figurative language and its effect)"]}]},{id:4,title:"One In, One Out",short:"Functions",parts:[{std:["8.F.A.1"],dur:165,dir:"Use the three tables and Graph D. Answer out loud, and use the definition of a function in every answer.",desc:"understanding a function as a rule that assigns exactly one output to each input, and reading the graph of a function",html:()=>lead("A function is a rule that gives each input exactly one output. These are four sets of records from the bloom project."),fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="flex-direction:column;align-items:center;gap:4px">${tableA()}${tableB()}${tableC()}${pointGraph()}</div>`,qs:["Is Table A a function? Is Table B a function? Use the definition in each answer.","Is Table C a function? Explain.","Is the set of points in Graph D a function? How can you tell by looking at the graph?","A rule says: to each tree in the orchard, assign the day it first bloomed. Is that a function? Now turn it around: to each day, assign the tree that bloomed on it. Is that a function?","A seedling follows the rule: each number of days after planting, d, gets a height of 2.5d + 4 centimetres. Find the output when d = 6 and write the ordered pair. Then describe what the graph of this function looks like."],key:["Table A is a function. Each input, 0, 7, 9, and 11 days, appears once and has exactly one output. Table B is also a function. The inputs 1, 2, 3, and 4 each appear once with one output, and the fact that trees 1 and 2 share the output April 24 does not matter, because the definition only forbids one input from having two outputs. Partial: one of the two tables answered is approaching. (8.F.A.1, the definition of a function)","No. The input 46 degrees appears twice, once with April 24 and once with April 14, so that input does not get exactly one output. These four rows are taken from the scatter plot on the later stop, and the April 14 spring is the odd one there too. Partial: no with no account of the repeated input is approaching. (8.F.A.1)","No. The input 5 has two points, (5, 6) and (5, 11), so one input has two outputs. By eye, a vertical line drawn at 5 would cross the graph twice, and the graph of a function is crossed at most once by any vertical line. Or: naming the vertical line test counts as the by-eye reason. (8.F.A.1, the graph of a function)","The first rule is a function, because each tree has exactly one first bloom day. The second is not, because more than one tree can bloom on the same day, so that input would be assigned several outputs. Partial: one of the two rules answered is approaching. (8.F.A.1, deciding whether a rule is a function)","19 centimetres, because 2.5 × 6 = 15 and 15 + 4 = 19. The ordered pair is (6, 19). The graph is the set of all ordered pairs (d, 2.5d + 4), and because the height goes up by the same 2.5 for every extra day, those pairs lie on a straight line that rises. Partial: the output and the pair right with nothing said about the graph is approaching. (8.F.A.1, the graph is the set of ordered pairs)"]}]},{id:5,title:"Reading the Record",short:"The Article",parts:[{std:["RI.8.1"],dur:140,dir:"Read the article to yourself. Then answer out loud. Read me the exact words that prove each answer.",desc:"citing the strongest evidence from an informational text for an explicit answer and for inferences",html:artText,qs:["What is phenology? Read me the exact words.","How far back does the Kyoto record reach, and where did it come from? Read me the words.","Which single sentence most strongly supports the claim that a bloom date is driven by accumulated heat rather than by the calendar? Pick A, B, or C and tell me why. A: “Two springs that begin on the same first of March can run two weeks apart. B: “Many temperate plants begin to develop once the daily average temperature rises past about 50 degrees Fahrenheit? what matters after that is the total warmth accumulated, not the number of days that have passed. C: “The record of cherry blossom dates kept at Kyoto reaches back more than 1,200 years.”","The article says “No single tree shows that.” Read me the words that explain why not.","Linnea is the second person ever to keep this record. Use the article to say what the single biggest risk to the value of those forty-two years is, and read me the strongest words for it."],key:["The study of when recurring natural events happen. Reads “The study of when recurring natural events happen is called phenology.” (RI.8.1, explicit evidence)","More than 1,200 years, assembled first from court diaries and later from official observation. Reads “The record of cherry blossom dates kept at Kyoto reaches back more than 1,200 years, assembled first from court diaries and later from official observation.” (RI.8.1)","B. It states the mechanism directly: a threshold temperature and then accumulated warmth rather than elapsed days. A is a consequence of B and only shows that two springs differ, without saying why. C is about the length of a record and has nothing to do with heat. (RI.8.1, strongest evidence)","Reads “The signal is small, the year-to-year scatter is large, and one late frost can move a date by a week. It appears only when a record is long, or when many records are pooled.” (RI.8.1)","That her version of the rule will drift from his, so the series becomes two records rather than one. Strongest: “A date is a measurement, and a measurement needs a definition that does not drift,” and “A record kept for thirty years to one rule and eleven years to a slightly looser one is not a forty-one year record. It is two short ones laid end to end, and the join is exactly where a trend would appear to begin.” The article never mentions Linnea, so this has to be put together. (RI.8.1, inference with the strongest support)"]}]},{id:6,title:"Straight Line or Not",short:"Linear?",parts:[{std:["8.F.A.3"],dur:175,dir:"Use the table for numbers 3 and 4. Work on your paper and tell your teacher each answer. Sketch number 2.",desc:"interpreting y = mx + b as a linear function and giving examples of functions that are not linear",html:()=>lead("A seedling grows one way and duckweed spreads another. Only one of them is a linear function."),fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${duckTable()}</div>`,qs:["A seedling is 4 centimetres tall when it is measured for the first time and grows 2.5 centimetres a week, so its height is h = 2.5w + 4. Is that a linear function? Name m and b and say what each one means here.","Sketch the graph of h = 2.5w + 4 from w = 0 to w = 6. Name two points that are on your line.","Look at the table. Is the duckweed cover a linear function of the day? Work out the changes and explain.","Give an example of a function that is not linear, other than the one in the table, and say how you know it is not linear.","Which of these define linear functions? (a) y = 9 − 4x (b) y = x<sup>2</sup> − 1 (c) y = 6 (d) y = 12 ÷ x (e) 2x + 5y = 20","A seed tray has 180 empty cells and a sower fills 15 of them a minute, so the number left empty is c = 180 − 15t. Is it linear? What do the 180 and the −15 mean, and what does the graph look like?"],key:["Yes. It has the form y = mx + b, so its graph is a straight line. m = 2.5 is the slope, the centimetres added each week. b = 4 is the value when w = 0, the height at the first measurement. Partial: yes with one of m and b not explained is approaching. (8.F.A.3, interpreting y = mx + b)","A straight line from (0, 4) up to (6, 19). Two points, such as (0, 4) and (6, 19), or (1, 6.5), (2, 9), (4, 14). Check: 2.5 × 6 = 15 and 15 + 4 = 19. Partial: a correct line with only one point named is approaching. (8.F.A.3, the graph of a linear function is a straight line)","No, it is not linear. The day goes up by 2 each time, but the cover changes by 6 − 3 = 3, then 12 − 6 = 6, then 24 − 12 = 12. Equal steps in the input do not give equal changes in the output, so the rate of change is not constant, the graph curves upward, and the rule cannot be written as y = mx + b. The cover doubles every two days, which makes it exponential rather than linear. Or: sketch the four pairs and show they curve. Partial: no with one difference worked out is approaching. (8.F.A.3, recognizing a function that is not linear)","Any correct example with a reason, such as: the area of a square plot, A = s<sup>2</sup>, where s = 1, 2, 3, 4 gives 1, 4, 9, 16 and the differences 3, 5, 7 are not equal, so the graph curves. Also fine: y = 3<sup>x</sup>, y = 1 ÷ x, or the volume of a cube. The student has to say why it is not linear, not just name it. (8.F.A.3, giving examples of functions that are not linear)","Linear: (a), with m = −4 and b = 9; (c), with m = 0 and b = 6, which graphs as a horizontal line; and (e), because solving for y gives y = −0.4x + 4. Not linear: (b), because x is squared, and (d), because x is in the denominator. Partial: (c) and (e) are the two usually missed, so three or four of the five sorted right is approaching. (8.F.A.3)","Linear. b = 180 is the number of empty cells at t = 0, and m = −15 means 15 fewer empty cells each minute, so the line falls as t goes up. The graph is a straight line starting at (0, 180) and reaching 0 at t = 12, because 180 ÷ 15 = 12. Partial: linear with one of the two numbers explained is approaching. (8.F.A.3, interpreting m and b in context)"]}]},{id:7,title:"Weigh the Memo",short:"The Memo",parts:[{std:["RI.8.8"],dur:140,dir:"Read the memo to yourself. Then answer out loud.",desc:"delineating an argument and judging whether the reasoning is sound and the evidence relevant and sufficient",html:memoText,qs:["What is Councilor Sandoval's claim? What reasons does she give for it?","Which reason rests on the strongest evidence? Explain why that evidence is both relevant and sufficient.","One sentence in the memo is irrelevant evidence. Which one, and why does it not belong in this argument?","Two parts of the argument rest on weak reasoning. Name one of them and tell me what is wrong with the thinking."],key:["Claim: the two acres between the mill and the creek should be planted as a community orchard this spring rather than restored as a wet meadow. Reasons: use, because 48 trees would supply the food pantry with about 9,000 pounds of fruit a year from ground that now produces nothing edible; cost, $31,000 plus about $4,200 a year against $58,000 for the meadow; time, productive in year four against eight to twelve years for the meadow to establish; and opinion, twenty-four of twenty-six people asked preferred fruit trees. Needs the claim and at least two reasons. (RI.8.8, delineating the argument)","Either the cost reason or the time reason, with the explanation. The cost reason is relevant because money is what the council is being asked to spend, and sufficient because it prices both options and separates the one-time cost from the yearly one, so a reader can compare them without being told the answer. The time reason is relevant because both plans are being judged on what the field will be, and sufficient because it gives a figure for each and names where the meadow figure came from, the meadow's own application. (RI.8.8, relevant and sufficient evidence)","The sentence that the mill building is on the national register. The register status of a neighbouring building has no bearing on whether the field should hold trees or meadow, so it supports neither the claim nor any of the four reasons. (RI.8.8, recognizing irrelevant evidence)","Either one. The Ashby point: a drop in pantry use the year after an orchard was planted does not show the orchard caused it, and the memo contradicts itself here, because its own third reason says an orchard is not productive until year four, so a 2019 orchard had no fruit to give anybody in 2020. Or the opinion reason: the twenty-six people asked were the ones who came to an orchard planning meeting, so they are the residents most in favour of an orchard and not a fair sample of the town, and twenty-four out of a self-selected twenty-six is not evidence about what the town wants. Needs the part named and what is wrong with the reasoning. (RI.8.8, soundness of reasoning)"]}]},{id:8,title:"Twelve Springs",short:"Scatter Plot",parts:[{std:["8.SP.A.1"],dur:175,dir:"Use the scatter plot. Answer out loud. Say what each number on the plot stands for before you use it.",desc:"interpreting a scatter plot of two measurements, describing the association, clustering, and outliers",html:()=>lead("Each dot is one spring. Across the bottom is the average March temperature that year. Up the side is the day of April on which the mill tree reached first bloom, so a lower dot means an earlier bloom."),fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${bloomPlot()}</div>`,qs:["Describe the association between the March temperature and the bloom date. Is it positive or negative, and is it linear?","Is there an outlier? Name the point, say what makes it one, and say what you would check before deciding whether to leave it in.","Do the points form one group or more than one? Say what you see.","A new spring has a March averaging 52 degrees. Use the pattern to estimate the bloom date, and then say why that is only an estimate.","A second plot is made of total April rainfall against bloom date, and the points sit in a cloud with no upward or downward drift at all. What is that plot telling you?"],key:["Negative, and roughly linear. As the average March temperature rises, the bloom day falls, so warmer Marches go with earlier blooms. Eleven of the twelve points fall close to a single straight band from about (41, 30) down to (51, 19), which is a drop of about one day of April for each degree. Partial: negative named with nothing said about linear, or the reverse, is approaching. (8.SP.A.1, negative linear association)","Yes, the point at 46 degrees and April 14. It sits about ten days below every other point near that temperature, and there is a second point at 46 degrees that blooms on April 24, so the two cannot both fit the pattern. Before deciding, check that year's notes: whether the same rule was applied, whether the observer or the viewing spot changed, whether the temperature came from the same thermometer, and whether something the plot does not show, such as an unusually warm February, explains it. An outlier is a reason to look, not a reason to delete. Partial: the point named with nothing to check before deciding is approaching. (8.SP.A.1, outliers)","One group, plus one point off on its own. There is no second cluster: the eleven points are spread fairly evenly along the band rather than gathering into two separate clumps. (8.SP.A.1, clustering)","About April 18. The band falls about 1.1 days for each degree, and from the point at (51, 19) one more degree takes it to about 17.9, which rounds to April 18. It is only an estimate because 52 degrees is beyond every March in the data, so nothing in the plot shows that the pattern keeps going, and the year-to-year scatter means a single spring can sit several days off the band anyway. Or: draw a trend line and read it at 52 degrees. Partial: an estimate in range with no account of why it is only an estimate is approaching. (8.SP.A.1, using the pattern)","That there is no association between April rainfall and the bloom date in this record. Knowing the rainfall would not help you guess the bloom day. It does not prove rain has no effect on anything, only that these two measurements do not move together here. Partial: no association named with no account of what that does and does not prove is approaching. (8.SP.A.1, no association)"]}]},{id:9,title:"Clause Work",short:"Grammar",parts:[{std:["L.8.1"],dur:140,dir:"A verbal is a verb form doing another job. Answer out loud. When you fix a sentence, say the whole sentence the correct way.",desc:"verbals, active and passive voice, mood, and fixing a shift in voice",qs:["“Keeping the record takes about twenty minutes a day.” Which word is the verbal? What kind is it, and what job does it do in the sentence?","“The notebook, held together by a rubber band, went back forty-one years.” Find the verbal and name its kind. Who or what does it describe?","“Her job was to stand at the gate and look.” Find the verbal and name its kind.","Say this sentence in active voice: “The notebook was handed to Linnea by Mr. Okpara.”","Fix the verb: “I wish the serviceberry was easier to see from the road.”","Fix the shift in voice: “Linnea checked the tree every morning, and then the date was written in the notebook by her.”","Name the mood of this sentence: “Write the date the evening you see it.”"],key:["Keeping. Gerund. With its phrase, “Keeping the record,” it is the subject of the sentence, so it acts as a noun. (L.8.1a, verbals)","held. Participle. It describes the notebook, so it acts as an adjective. (L.8.1a, verbals)","to stand. Infinitive. The phrase is “to stand at the gate and look,” with the second verb sharing the same to. (L.8.1a, verbals)","Mr. Okpara handed the notebook to Linnea. (L.8.1b, active and passive voice)","I wish the serviceberry were easier to see from the road. A wish about something that is not the case takes were. (L.8.1c, subjunctive mood)","Linnea checked the tree every morning, and then she wrote the date in the notebook. Both verbs are now active. (L.8.1d, shift in voice)","Imperative. It gives a direction. (L.8.1c, mood)"]}]},{id:10,title:"Source Desk",short:"Sources",parts:[{std:["W.8.8"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:145,dir:"Read both sources. Answer numbers 1 and 2 out loud. Do numbers 3 and 4 on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"judging sources, choosing search terms, fixing plagiarism, and citing",html:()=>SOURCES()+`<ol class="qlist"><li>The two sources disagree about how much earlier spring has moved. Which source would you trust? Give two reasons from the source information.</li><li>You want one more source. Which search terms are best, and why? A: spring. B: first bloom date trend single observer phenology record. C: when should I plant my garden.</li><li>Spot the plagiarism. A student's report says, with no quotation marks and no source named: <em>Across the 41 years, first bloom moved earlier by an average of 2.4 days per decade.</em> Fix it on your paper. Quote it correctly, or paraphrase it and name the author.</li><li>Write a citation for Source A with the author, title, publisher, and year.</li></ol>`,key:["Source A. Two reasons, such as: it names an author who is a plant ecologist, it comes from a state phenology network, it carries a date and is recent, it gives a measured figure with a stated span, and it states its own limits, both that no ten-year window is significant and that four years are flagged as recorded under a changed procedure. Source B is an undated post by an unknown user who writes “definitely like” and “you can just tell,” which is a feeling and not a measurement. Full credit for two reasons drawn from the source lines. (W.8.8, credibility and accuracy)","B. It uses the key words of the research question, so the results will be about bloom dates, trends over time, and the kind of record this one is. A is far too broad and C is a different question. (W.8.8, search terms)","Either fix earns the check. Quote: Dr. Yusuf Adeyemi reports that across the 41 years, “first bloom moved earlier by an average of 2.4 days per decade.” Paraphrase: According to Dr. Yusuf Adeyemi, the Marrow Creek record shows first bloom arriving about two and a half days earlier every ten years. The student must name the source either way, and the quotation must carry quotation marks. (W.8.8, quoting or paraphrasing without plagiarism)","Has all four parts. Example: Adeyemi, Yusuf. “Forty-One Years of First Bloom at Marrow Creek: Digitizing a Single-Observer Record.” State Phenology Network, 2025. Any clear, consistent order is fine. (W.8.8, citation)"]}]},{id:11,title:"Field Desk",short:"Write",parts:[{std:["W.8.3"],n:7,label:"checks met",dur:240,dir:"Write on your paper for about 5 minutes. Then hold it up or read it to your teacher.",desc:"a narrative writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">You have been given a job that somebody else has done the same way for forty years, and a rule for doing it that only makes sense once you have done it wrong. This is the morning you think you have it, and you are not sure. Somebody is waiting to hear. Write the story of that morning. Write 10 to 12 sentences.</p>${checklist(["Set the scene: where you are, who is telling the story, and what you can see and hear","Tell the events in the order they happen","Include at least two lines of dialogue","Use details the reader can see, hear, or feel","Use time words that signal a shift: at first, a few minutes later, by the time, finally","End by showing what the narrator felt or understood by the end of the morning"])}`,key:["Establishes a situation and a point of view and introduces the narrator and other characters (W.8.3a)","Events unfold in an order that is natural and logical (W.8.3a)","Uses narrative techniques such as dialogue, pacing, description, or reflection (W.8.3b)","Includes at least two lines of dialogue that move the story along (W.8.3b)","Uses transition words and phrases that show sequence and shifts in time (W.8.3c)","Uses precise words, descriptive details, and sensory language (W.8.3d)","Ends with a conclusion that follows from and reflects on the events (W.8.3e)"]}]},{id:12,title:"Brief the Council",short:"Brief",parts:[{std:["SL.8.4"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:140,dir:"Plan for 1 minute. Then talk for 1 to 2 minutes. Look at the camera and speak clearly.",desc:"a 1 to 2 minute spoken briefing that presents a claim and findings with evidence from a data table",html:()=>`<p class="big">The council has to decide what the two acres between the mill and the creek become. It can pay for one plan, not both. Your survey produced the table below.</p>${bigTable(["","Community orchard","Wet meadow"],[["Cost to plant or restore","$31,000","$58,000"],["Cost each year after that","about $4,200","about $900 after year three"],["Years until it does the job it is for","4","8 to 12"],["What it produces","about 9,000 lb of fruit a year for the food pantry at full size","no crop"],["Flooding","no change to how the field holds water","holds an estimated 240,000 gallons in a large storm, which is water the creek does not get at once"],["Who maintains it","volunteers, with a paid pruner twice a year","town crew for three years, then almost nothing"],["Worth knowing","the field floods in two Marches out of five","the two acres sit between the mill and the creek bank"]],"Two plans for the creek field")}<p class="big" style="font-size:1.5rem">Brief the council. Say which plan to fund, give your strongest evidence from the table, name the drawback of your choice, and end with what you need the council to do.</p><div class="row center" style="margin-top:16px;gap:14px">${["My recommendation","The evidence","The drawback","What I need from the council"].map(x=>`<span class="pill" style="font-size:1.1rem">${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`,key:["States a clear recommendation up front and stays focused on it. Either plan can be recommended. The orchard case rests on $31,000 against $58,000, on year four against eight to twelve, and on about 9,000 pounds of fruit for the pantry. The meadow case rests on the field flooding in two Marches out of five, on 240,000 gallons held back from the creek, and on about $900 a year after year three against $4,200. (SL.8.4)","Emphasizes the most important points in a focused, coherent order rather than reading the whole table out (SL.8.4)","Backs the recommendation with relevant evidence and sound reasoning from the table, with the numbers used accurately (SL.8.4)","Uses well-chosen details, including the drawback. For the orchard, the field floods in two Marches out of five and the yearly cost is nearly five times the meadow's, and nothing is held back from the creek. For the meadow, it produces no food, costs almost twice as much to start, and does not do its job for eight to twelve years. (SL.8.4)","Appropriate eye contact with the camera, adequate volume, and clear pronunciation for 1 to 2 minutes (SL.8.4)"]}]},{id:13,title:"The Lines That Move It",short:"Dialogue",parts:[{std:["RL.8.3"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Look back at the story. Answer out loud, and read me the exact words you are using.",desc:"analyzing how particular lines and incidents propel the action, reveal character, or provoke a decision",html:()=>story(),qs:["Mr. Okpara says, You will get it wrong. How does that line move the action forward?","On the twenty-second Linnea sees a smear of something at the top and writes nothing. What decision does that incident provoke, and what does it reveal about her?","The last sentence turns on a question nobody asks out loud. Which earlier incident sets it up, and what does it reveal about Mr. Okpara?"],key:["It takes being wrong off the table as a reason not to start. He does not reassure her, he tells her the first three years are wrong and that everybody's are, which makes the wrong years part of the method rather than a failure. That is what sends her down on the fifteenth and on every day after it, through six days of nothing (RL.8.3, a line of dialogue propelling the action)","She decides it does not count, and writes nothing. What reveals her is the reason: she can only see it because she knows exactly where to look, and the rule says white, from the gate. She would rather record nothing than record something that only works if you already know the answer. She has taken the rule as a definition and not as a target (RL.8.3, an incident revealing a character)","He stopped putting the g after the date and started leaving the line blank, which is the whole reason a fourteen-year-old is at his kitchen table in March. He hands the job over without ever saying he is handing it over, and the rubber band he takes off and puts back about every forty seconds shows how much of this he is not saying. The question of whose notebook it is now is the one he has left her to ask (RL.8.3, an incident provoking a decision)"]}]},{id:14,title:"Two Accounts of Ashby",short:"Conflict",parts:[{std:["RI.8.9"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Read both texts. Answer out loud, and quote the words you are relying on.",desc:"analyzing two conflicting texts on one topic and separating where they disagree on fact from where they disagree on interpretation",html:()=>ashbyPair(),qs:["Name one point the two texts agree on, and state it exactly.","Where do they disagree on a matter of fact? Quote both.","Where do they disagree on a matter of interpretation, and what in each text leads it there?"],key:["The 18 percent figure. Both say pantry visits in Ashby fell by 18 percent in the year after the orchard was planted. Text 1 dates the planting to the spring of 2019 and the drop to 2020; Text 2 gives no years and only says the drop came in the year after the orchard went in, so the agreement is about the size of the drop and when it fell relative to the planting (RI.8.9)","Whether the orchard was producing food in 2020. Text 1 says its first harvest of any size was in 2023 and that before that the trees produced almost nothing. Text 2 treats Ashby as a town where an orchard was followed by a drop in pantry demand and counts reduced pantry need among an orchard's benefits, which only makes sense if the orchard was feeding somebody. A tree planted in 2019 producing nothing until 2023 cannot have fed anyone in 2020 (RI.8.9, disagreement on a matter of fact)","The cause of the drop. Text 1 reads it as the pantry move: out of the old fire station, into an annex with no parking, in the same January, and the town's own report names the move. Text 2 reads it as the orchard, and gets there by setting Ashby beside several other towns and calling the pattern strong. One text is explaining one town from the inside and has a competing cause in front of it. The other is reading across towns and sees a shape. Count it correct when the student names the two readings and says what each text is doing that produces them (RI.8.9, disagreement on a matter of interpretation)"]}]},{id:15,title:"Who Keeps the Record",short:"Argue",parts:[{std:["W.8.1"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:80,dir:"Write on your paper. Then hold it up or read it to your teacher.",desc:"an argument supporting a claim with clear reasons and relevant evidence",html:()=>`<p class="big">Dr. Adeyemi's report flags the last four years of the Marrow Creek notebook as recorded under a changed procedure. The town has to decide what happens next. It can leave the record with one observer who knows the tree, or hand it to the middle school science class, where a different student takes it each year to a written rule.</p><p class="big">Write an argument for one of the two. Write 6 to 8 sentences.</p>${checklist(["Open by naming the question and stating your claim","Acknowledge what the other choice has going for it","Give reasons backed by evidence from the article, used accurately","Use words and phrases that show how your claim, reasons and evidence connect","Keep a formal style, and end with a conclusion that follows from your argument"])}`,key:["Introduces the claim and acknowledges the alternative rather than pretending it has nothing to it (W.8.1a)","Supports the claim with logical reasoning and relevant evidence from the article, used accurately. The article's own points are that a measurement needs a definition that does not drift, that thirty years to one rule and eleven to a looser one is two short records laid end to end, and that the join is exactly where a trend would appear to begin (W.8.1b)","Shows understanding of the topic. A student arguing for the class has to deal with the fact that a new observer each year is a new chance for the rule to drift. A student arguing for one observer has to deal with the four flagged years (W.8.1b)","Uses words, phrases and clauses to show how the claim, the reasons, the evidence and the counterclaim connect (W.8.1c)","Keeps a formal style and ends with a conclusion that follows from and supports the argument (W.8.1d and W.8.1e)"]}]},{id:16,title:"A Letter to the Network",short:"Explain",parts:[{std:["W.8.2"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:75,dir:"Write on your paper. Then hold it up or read it to your teacher.",desc:"a short business letter explaining what the record is and what a user of it needs to know",html:()=>`<p class="big">The State Phenology Network has asked Marrow Creek to describe the notebook before the data goes into the national archive. Write the letter. Explain what the record is, how it was taken, and what somebody using it would need to know about it.</p>${checklist(["Lay it out as a business letter: a date, who it is to, a greeting, paragraphs, and a sign-off","Open by saying what the letter is about, then organize it into clear parts","Develop it with facts, definitions and concrete details from the article and the story","Use the vocabulary the subject needs, and explain any term the reader may not share","Keep a formal style and end with a conclusion that follows from the information"])}`,key:["Laid out as a simple business letter, with a date, an addressee, a greeting, paragraphs and a sign-off. Anything close to a standard layout counts; a page of unbroken prose does not (W.8.2, career development documents)","Introduces the topic clearly and organizes the information into parts, such as what the record is, how it was taken, and what a user should watch for (W.8.2a)","Develops it with facts, definitions and concrete details, used accurately: forty-one years, one tree, one observer, the rule that first bloom means white seen from the gate, the four years marked with a g, the change of procedure (W.8.2b)","Uses precise language and the subject's vocabulary, such as phenology, observer, record and procedure, and explains any term the reader might not share (W.8.2d)","Keeps a formal style and ends with a concluding statement that follows from the information, such as what the archive should note alongside the data (W.8.2e and W.8.2f)"]}]},{id:17,title:"Rational or Not",short:"Roots",parts:[{std:["8.NS.A.1","8.EE.A.2"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Work on your paper. Say your answers out loud.",desc:"sorting rational from irrational numbers, converting a repeating decimal to a fraction, and using root symbols",html:()=>lead("Six numbers turned up in the bloom data: 0.375, 2/9, the square root of 2, the square root of 49, 0.474747 repeating, and pi."),qs:["Sort the six into rational and irrational. For each rational one, say what its decimal expansion does.","Convert 0.474747 repeating into a fraction. Show the method.","Solve x squared = 49 and y cubed = 64 using root symbols. Then evaluate the square root of 81 and the cube root of 27. Why is the square root of 2 irrational rather than just a very long decimal?"],key:["Rational: 0.375, which terminates; 2/9, which is 0.2222 repeating; the square root of 49, which is 7, a whole number; and 0.474747 repeating. Irrational: the square root of 2 and pi, whose expansions never terminate and never fall into a repeat. Partial: the six sorted right with the decimal behaviour not described is approaching. (8.NS.A.1)","47/99 (8.NS.A.1). Let x = 0.474747 repeating. The repeat is two digits long, so multiply by 100: 100x = 47.474747 repeating. Subtracting gives 99x = 47, so x = 47/99. Check by dividing: 47 / 99 = 0.474747. Or: name it from the pattern, since a two-digit repeat sits over 99. Partial: 47/99 with no method shown is approaching.","x = plus or minus the square root of 49, so x = 7 or x = -7. Both roots, not just the positive one. y = the cube root of 64 = 4. The square root of 81 is 9 and the cube root of 27 is 3 (8.EE.A.2). The square root of 2 is irrational because no fraction of two whole numbers squares to 2, so its decimal cannot terminate and cannot settle into a repeating block. Length alone never makes a number irrational: 2/9 goes on forever too and is rational. Partial: three of the four values right is approaching, and x = 7 alone leaves the negative root out."]}]},{id:18,title:"Two Planting Crews",short:"Slope",parts:[{std:["8.EE.B.5"],n:3,dur:55,dir:"Work on your paper. Say your answers out loud.",desc:"reading a unit rate as the slope of a proportional graph and comparing two relationships in different forms",html:()=>lead("Crew A plants trees at the rate t = 18h, where h is hours worked and t is trees planted. Crew B's planting is the graph below."),fig:()=>crewGraph(),qs:["What is Crew B's unit rate, and what is the slope of its graph?","Which crew plants faster, and by how much per hour?","If Crew A's line were drawn on the same axes, how would the two lines compare, and what would the difference mean?"],key:["15 trees per hour. The plotted point is (4, 60), so 60 / 4 = 15, and the line runs through the origin, so the slope is 15. The unit rate and the slope are the same number. Or: take the rise over the run between any two gridlines on the line. Partial: 15 with no link made to the slope is approaching.","Crew A, by 3 trees an hour. Crew A's equation gives 18 trees an hour, which is the coefficient of h, and Crew B's graph gives 15. 18 - 15 = 3. Partial: the right crew with no difference per hour given is approaching.","Both lines would start at the origin, because neither crew plants anything in zero hours. Crew A's line would be steeper. The steeper line is the faster crew, because a bigger slope means more trees for each hour of work. At 5 hours Crew A is at 90 and Crew B is at 75. Partial: the steeper line named with nothing said about what the difference means is approaching."]}]},{id:19,title:"Where the Lines Meet",short:"Systems",parts:[{std:["8.EE.C.8"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Work on your paper. Show your steps and check each answer.",desc:"solving pairs of simultaneous linear equations and saying what a solution means on a graph",html:()=>lead("Two plans for the same field can be written as a pair of equations."),qs:["Solve y = 2x + 1 and y = -x + 7.","Solve 3x + 2y = 16 and x - 2y = 0.","What does the solution of a pair mean on a graph? How many solutions do y = 3x - 2 and y = 3x + 5 have, and why?"],key:["x = 2, y = 5. Substituting the first into the second: 2x + 1 = -x + 7, so 3x = 6 and x = 2, then y = 2(2) + 1 = 5. Check in the second: -2 + 7 = 5. Or: elimination, or graphing both lines and reading the crossing point. Partial: x = 2 with no y is approaching.","x = 4, y = 2. Adding the two equations eliminates y: 4x = 16, so x = 4. Then 4 - 2y = 0 gives y = 2. Check in the first: 3(4) + 2(2) = 12 + 4 = 16. Or: substitution, x = 2y from the second equation into the first. Partial: x = 4 with no y is approaching.","It is the point where the two lines cross, the one pair of values that satisfies both equations at once. The second pair has no solution: both lines have slope 3 but different intercepts, so they are parallel and never meet. Setting them equal gives -2 = 5, which is never true. Partial: one of the two halves answered is approaching."]}]},{id:20,title:"Move the Triangle",short:"Transform",parts:[{std:["8.G.A.3"],n:4,dur:75,dir:"Use the grid. Work on your paper. Say the coordinates out loud.",desc:"describing translations, reflections, rotations and dilations of a figure using coordinates",html:()=>lead("Triangle ABC has vertices A(1, 2), B(4, 2) and C(4, 6)."),fig:()=>triGrid(),qs:["Translate the triangle 3 left and 1 up. Give the coordinates of the image.","Reflect the original triangle across the x-axis, then reflect that image across the y-axis. Give the coordinates after each step.","Go back to the original triangle. Rotate it 90 degrees counterclockwise about the origin. Give the rule for what happens to a point, and give the image coordinates.","Dilate the original triangle from the origin by a factor of 2. Give the image coordinates, and say what happens to the side lengths and to the angles."],key:["A'(-2, 3), B'(1, 3), C'(1, 7). A translation of 3 left and 1 up subtracts 3 from every x and adds 1 to every y. The shape and the size do not change. Or: count the squares on the grid instead of using the rule. Partial: two of the three points right is approaching.","Across the x-axis the y changes sign: (1, -2), (4, -2), (4, -6). Across the y-axis the x changes sign: (-1, -2), (-4, -2), (-4, -6). The two reflections together are the same as a half turn about the origin, which sends (x, y) to (-x, -y). Or: do the half turn in one move, (x, y) to (−x, −y), as long as the student shows it matches. Partial: one of the two steps right is approaching.","The rule is (x, y) goes to (−y, x). A'(−2, 1), B'(−2, 4), C'(−6, 4). Check one: C is (4, 6), so the image is (−6, 4). A rotation keeps every side length and every angle, so the image is congruent to the original. Or: trace the triangle and turn the paper a quarter turn to the left, or call it a 270° clockwise turn. Partial: the rule right with a point wrong, or three right points with no rule, is approaching.","A''(2, 4), B''(8, 4), C''(8, 12). A dilation from the origin by a factor of 2 doubles both coordinates. Every side length doubles, so AB goes from 3 to 6 and BC from 4 to 8, but every angle keeps its measure. The image is the same shape, twice the size. Partial: the points and the side lengths right with nothing said about the angles is approaching."]}]},{id:21,title:"Across the Corner",short:"Pythagoras",parts:[{std:["8.G.B.7"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Work on your paper. Say your answers out loud and keep the units.",desc:"applying the Pythagorean theorem to find unknown lengths in two and three dimensions",html:()=>lead("Three lengths have to be worked out at the mill field."),qs:["A ladder leans against the mill wall. It reaches 12 feet up the wall and its foot is 5 feet out from the wall. How long is the ladder?","A rectangular bed is 9 feet by 12 feet. How long is a path laid straight across it, corner to corner?","A storage box is 6 feet by 8 feet by 10 feet. What is the longest straight rod that will fit inside it, corner to opposite corner?"],key:["13 feet. 5 squared + 12 squared = 25 + 144 = 169, and the square root of 169 is 13. The wall and the ground meet at a right angle, so the ladder is the hypotenuse. Or: recognise the 5, 12, 13 triple. Partial: 169 found with no square root taken is approaching.","15 feet. 9 squared + 12 squared = 81 + 144 = 225, and the square root of 225 is 15. Or: recognise the 3, 4, 5 triple scaled by 3. Partial: 21 feet from adding the two sides does not count.","About 14.1 feet, exactly 10 times the square root of 2. Do it in two steps: the diagonal across the base is the square root of (6 squared + 8 squared) = the square root of 100 = 10 feet. Then the space diagonal is the square root of (10 squared + 10 squared) = the square root of 200, which is about 14.14 feet. Doing it in one step, the square root of (36 + 64 + 100), gives the same 200. Partial: the base diagonal, 10 feet, on its own is approaching."]}]},{id:22,title:"Rules for New Ground",short:"Founding",parts:[{std:["HSS-8.1"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the land ordinances as a precedent for how the nation would grow",html:()=>`<p class="big">Before the Constitution existed, the Confederation Congress decided what to do with the land north and west of the Ohio River.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Law</th><th>What it set up</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Land Ordinance of 1785</td><td>surveyed the territory into townships of 36 square mile sections, with one section in each township reserved to pay for a school</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Northwest Ordinance of 1787</td><td>set a path to statehood: a territory with 60,000 free inhabitants could apply and would enter on equal footing with the original states</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Northwest Ordinance of 1787</td><td>guaranteed trial by jury and freedom of belief in the territory, and barred slavery from it</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("map",74)}${ico("sprout",74)}${ico("books",74)}</div>`,qs:["New states entered on equal footing with the old ones. Why was that choice unusual, and what would the alternative have been?","One section of every township was set aside to pay for a school. What does that tell you about what the Congress expected of the new settlements?","The 1787 ordinance barred slavery north of the Ohio and said nothing about south of it. Name one consequence of that line."],key:["Unusual because the normal practice of the day was for a country to hold new land as a colony, governed from the center and permanently junior. Equal footing meant the country would grow by adding full partners rather than dependencies, which is why the United States expanded without a colonial system on the continent. The alternative would have been western territories as provinces, taxed and governed without a vote. Partial: naming the equal footing rule with no alternative named is approaching. (HSS-8.1)","That schooling was treated as part of the basic equipment of a self governing community, not an extra. If voters decide, they have to be able to read, so Congress built a funding source into the survey itself before a single settler arrived. It is the Northwest Ordinance's most durable legacy in the Midwest. Partial: noting the school land with nothing said about voters needing to read is approaching. (HSS-8.1)","Any accurate consequence: it drew the first national line between free and slave territory and established that Congress could decide the question for a territory, which is exactly the power the Dred Scott decision denied seventy years later; it made the Ohio River a boundary that people escaping slavery aimed for; and it meant that every later territorial acquisition reopened the same argument. Partial: naming the ban with no consequence carried forward is approaching. (HSS-8.1)"]},{std:["HSS-8.2"],n:3,dur:100,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the compromises in the Constitutional Convention and the principles they traded",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three bargains struck in Philadelphia in 1787.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Bargain</th><th>What it settled</th></tr>
     <tr><td>The Great Compromise</td><td>a House apportioned by population and a Senate with two seats per state</td></tr>
     <tr><td>The Three Fifths Compromise</td><td>for both representation and direct taxes, three of every five enslaved people would be counted</td></tr>
     <tr><td>The commerce bargain</td><td>Congress could regulate trade by simple majority, could not tax exports, and could not ban the importing of enslaved people before 1808</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("books",74)}${ico("purpleFlower",74)}${ico("coin",74)}</div>`,qs:["The Great Compromise gives a small state the same Senate power as a large one. Which principle does each half of Congress serve?","State plainly what the Three Fifths Compromise did, and who gained from it.","Explain why the delegates thought the commerce bargain was necessary, and what it cost."],key:["The House serves popular sovereignty: representation follows people, so a majority of the country can be a majority there. The Senate serves federalism: the states are parties to the union and each has an equal voice regardless of size. The two together let a document be ratified by both large and small states, and they still pull against each other in every Congress. Partial: one of the two chambers matched to its principle is approaching. (HSS-8.2)","It counted three of every five enslaved people when apportioning seats in the House and the electoral college, which gave slaveholding states more representatives and more electoral votes than their free population justified, while the people counted had no vote, no rights and no say. The gain went to slaveholders, and it is why the presidency and the House leaned toward the slaveholding states for decades. Partial: describing the count with nothing said about who gained and who had no say is approaching. (HSS-8.2)","The delegates from South Carolina and Georgia said plainly they would not join a union that could end the importing of enslaved people, and Northern delegates wanted the commerce power without a supermajority requirement, so each side got its priority. The cost was twenty more years of a trade that brought tens of thousands more people into slavery, and a Constitution that protected it by name without using the word. Partial: one of what each side got and what it cost is approaching. (HSS-8.2)"]},{std:["HSS-8.3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing freedom of the press and assembly as instruments of citizen participation",html:()=>`<p class="big">In 1735 a New York printer named John Peter Zenger was tried for libel for printing criticism of the colony's governor. Under the law of the time, truth was no defense: printing the criticism was the offense. His lawyer argued to the jury that a free people must be able to say what their governor does, and the jury acquitted him against the judge's instruction. Sixty years later the Sedition Act of 1798 made it a crime to publish false or malicious writing about the government, and about two dozen printers and editors, nearly all of them from the opposition party, were prosecuted under it. The act expired in 1801 and the men convicted were pardoned.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("pencil",74)}${ico("books",74)}${ico("hands",74)}</div>`,qs:["Why would a jury acquit a printer whom the judge had told them was guilty under the law?","The Sedition Act was passed seven years after the First Amendment. How did its supporters square the two?","Name two ways a free press lets a citizen take part in government, beyond simply reading the news."],key:["Because the jury judged the law itself unjust as applied, and in a criminal trial the verdict is theirs. The acquittal did not change the statute, but it established in practice that a New York jury would not convict a printer for truthful criticism, which made prosecutions useless. It is a case of participation by ordinary people restraining a government directly. Partial: naming the acquittal with nothing said about the law being unchanged is approaching. (HSS-8.3)","They argued the amendment barred prior censorship, meaning the government could not stop a thing from being printed, but did not bar punishing false and malicious statements after publication. The opposition answered that a government deciding which criticism is malicious is a government choosing its own critics, and the elections of 1800 largely settled the argument politically. Partial: one of the argument and the answer to it given is approaching. (HSS-8.3)","Any two: it reports what officials actually do, so voters can judge them; it carries a citizen's own argument to others through letters, columns and pamphlets; it exposes misconduct that no voter could discover alone; and it gives people who agree a way to find each other, which is how organizations and petitions get started. Partial: one function of the press named is approaching. (HSS-8.3)"]}]},{id:23,title:"Belief and Liberty",short:"New Nation",parts:[{std:["HSS-8.4"],n:4,dur:105,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing religious liberty and disestablishment as ideals of the new nation",html:()=>`<p class="big">At independence, most states had an established church: one denomination supported by taxes, with its clergy paid from public funds. Dismantling that took sixty years.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Year</th><th>Step</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1786</td><td>Virginia adopts the Statute for Religious Freedom, drafted by Jefferson and carried by Madison: no person may be compelled to support any religion, and belief is not a qualification for anything</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1787</td><td>Article VI of the Constitution bars any religious test for federal office</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1791</td><td>The First Amendment bars Congress from establishing a religion and protects its free exercise</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1833</td><td>Massachusetts, the last state to do so, ends tax support for its Congregational churches</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Baptists and Methodists, who had been taxed to support other denominations and sometimes jailed for preaching without a license, were among the hardest campaigners for all of this.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("books",74)}${ico("sprout",74)}${ico("hands",74)}</div>`,qs:["The First Amendment binds Congress. Massachusetts kept an established church until 1833. Explain how both were true at the same time.","Why would Baptists and Methodists campaign hardest for disestablishment, when a church might be expected to want public support?","Article VI bans a religious test for office. What would such a test have looked like, and what was being prevented?","Madison argued that religion would be stronger without government support, not weaker. Explain his reasoning."],key:["The First Amendment originally restricted only the national government, so it said nothing about what a state could do with its own taxes. Disestablishment at the state level had to be won state by state, and it was, ending in Massachusetts in 1833. The amendment was applied against the states only much later, through the Fourteenth Amendment. Partial: noting the state churches with nothing said about the amendment reaching only the national government is approaching. (HSS-8.4)","Because they were not the established denomination anywhere and were on the paying end of it: their members were taxed to support Congregational or Anglican ministers, and their own preachers were fined or jailed for lacking a license. Support from the state is only an advantage if the state picks you, which is exactly why a minority faith wants the government out of the business. Partial: naming their support with nothing said about being on the paying end is approaching. (HSS-8.4)","A requirement that an officeholder swear to a particular belief or belong to a particular church, which was ordinary in Britain and in several states. Banning it prevented the federal government from being closed to Catholics, Jews, Quakers, Baptists or anyone else by creed, and it meant public office rested on election or appointment rather than on belief. Partial: defining the test with nothing said about what banning it prevented is approaching. (HSS-8.4)","He argued that a church funded by taxes depends on politicians rather than on its own members, which makes its clergy comfortable and its congregations indifferent, and that it drags religion into every political fight. A church that must persuade people to join and to give has to be persuasive. The explosion of revivals and new denominations after disestablishment is evidence often cited for him. Partial: naming the independence of the church with nothing said about having to persuade members is approaching. (HSS-8.4)"]},{std:["HSS-8.5"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing Pinckney's Treaty and why access to the Mississippi drove early foreign policy",html:()=>`<p class="big">Spain held Louisiana and both banks of the lower Mississippi, including New Orleans, and closed the river to American shipping. A farmer in Tennessee or Kentucky could float a crop downriver in days or haul it over the Appalachians for weeks at a cost that ate the profit. Settlers began talking openly about dealing with Spain themselves, or leaving the Union. In 1795 Thomas Pinckney negotiated a treaty in which Spain opened the river to American traffic, allowed American goods to be stored at New Orleans without duty, and accepted the 31st parallel as the Florida boundary.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("wave",74)}${ico("wheat",74)}${ico("map",74)}</div>`,qs:["Western settlers were talking about leaving the Union over a river. Explain why a closed port was that serious.","Spain had refused for years and then agreed in 1795. What had changed?","What does this episode show about the connection between foreign policy and holding a country together?"],key:["Because the river was the only route by which a western crop could reach a market at a price worth growing it for. With New Orleans closed, a farmer's surplus was worth nothing, and a government that could not open it was providing no benefit worth loyalty. Economic access and political allegiance were the same question for people west of the mountains. Partial: naming the river with nothing said about loyalty following access is approaching. (HSS-8.5)","Spain had just made peace with France and was worried about a hostile Britain and about the possibility that Americans or the British would simply take the territory, and it had seen Jay's treaty suggest an Anglo-American understanding. A weaker Spain preferred to grant the river and fix a border than to risk losing everything, so American leverage came from Europe's wars rather than from American strength. Partial: crediting American strength is the misreading to watch for; the leverage came from Europe's wars, so that answer is approaching. (HSS-8.5)","That a national government has to deliver something its distant citizens need, or they will look elsewhere. The treaty bound the West to the Union more effectively than any argument could, and the same logic explains why Jefferson treated New Orleans as worth almost any price eight years later. Foreign policy here was internal policy. Partial: naming the treaty with nothing said about binding the West to the Union is approaching. (HSS-8.5)"]},{std:["HSS-8.6"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing interchangeable parts and mechanized production in the Northeast",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two ways to make a thousand muskets.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>By gunsmiths</th><th>By machine, Springfield and Harpers Ferry by the 1840s</th></tr>
     <tr><td>How a part was made</td><td>filed and fitted by hand to one gun</td><td>cut by a machine to a measured gauge</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Will a part from gun 7 fit gun 480?</td><td>no</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Who could do the work</td><td>a trained gunsmith</td><td>a worker trained on one machine</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Repair in the field</td><td>refit or replace the whole gun</td><td>swap in a spare part</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Samuel Slater, a mechanic who had worked in an English mill, reproduced British spinning machinery from memory in Rhode Island in 1790. By the 1850s the same approach was turning out clocks, sewing machines, shoes and farm tools.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("ruler",74)}${ico("box",74)}${ico("clock",74)}</div>`,qs:["Why does row two change what an army, and later a consumer, can do?","Row three looks like a small change and it reshaped the workforce. Explain how.","Slater brought the design from Britain, which forbade exporting it. What does that tell you about early American industry?"],key:["Because a repair no longer needs a craftsman who knows that particular object. An army can carry spare parts and keep guns in service, and later a buyer can replace a broken piece of a sewing machine or a clock by mail. Interchangeability is what makes mass produced goods maintainable, which is a large part of why they took over. Partial: naming the spare parts with nothing said about maintainability is approaching. (HSS-8.6)","It separated skill from the worker and put it into the machine and the gauge. A manufacturer no longer needed scarce, expensive gunsmiths and could hire from a much wider pool, including women and young workers, at lower wages. Output rose and the trained craftsman's bargaining position collapsed, which is the central labor story of the century. Partial: noting the lower wages with nothing said about skill moving into the machine is approaching. (HSS-8.6)","That it began by copying. The United States had capital, water power and a protected market but not the engineering knowledge, so it acquired that by recruiting British mechanics and reproducing designs that Britain was trying to keep. A strong answer notes that a developing economy usually imitates before it invents, and that American industry then went further with interchangeable parts than Britain did. Partial: calling it American invention is the misreading to watch for and is approaching. (HSS-8.6)"]}]},{id:24,title:"Fields, Trails, Parties",short:"Sections",parts:[{std:["HSS-8.7"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing why most white southerners, who owned no one, supported slavery",html:()=>`<p class="big">About three quarters of white Southern families held no one in slavery. Most farmed small plots in the hill country and the upcountry, grew their own food, traded locally and owed nothing to a planter. They still voted for slaveholding politicians and in 1861 most of their sons went to war.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("wheat",74)}${ico("tractor",74)}${ico("purpleFlower",74)}</div>`,qs:["Give two reasons a farmer who owned no one would still support the system.","Non slaveholding white southerners were also the people who got the fewest public schools, the fewest roads and the least from their state governments. Why did that not turn into opposition?","Some did oppose it. Where would you expect to find them, and why there?"],key:["Any two with reasoning: the hope of buying into it, since owning even one person was the recognized step up; the hiring out system and the local economy that ran on it; the belief that it kept them above someone, which a society organized by race offered them at no cost; fear of what emancipation would mean where Black southerners were a majority; and family, neighborhood and party loyalty to the men who ran the region. Partial: one reason named with reasoning is approaching. (HSS-8.7)","Because the political argument was organized around race rather than around class, and the people who ran the states kept it there. Attacking the planters meant attacking slavery, which the same argument had taught them to see as the guarantee of their own standing. A strong answer notices that the system paid them in status instead of in schools. Partial: noting the lack of schools with nothing said about how the argument was organized is approaching. (HSS-8.7)","In the upcountry and the mountains, where the soil and the terrain did not suit plantation crops and almost nobody held enslaved people, so the economic interest was absent. Eastern Tennessee, western Virginia and northern Alabama are the clearest cases, and western Virginia left the state and entered the Union as West Virginia in 1863. Partial: naming the regions with nothing said about soil or terrain is approaching. (HSS-8.7)"]},{std:["HSS-8.8"],n:3,dur:100,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the Mormon migration to the Great Basin as a case of western settlement",html:()=>`<p class="big">The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints was founded in New York in 1830. Its members were driven out of Ohio, then Missouri, then Illinois, where their founder Joseph Smith was killed by a mob in 1844. Brigham Young then led them west, out of the United States entirely as the boundary stood.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Year</th><th></th></tr>
     <tr><td>1846 to 1847</td><td>about 1,500 miles overland to the valley of the Great Salt Lake, then Mexican territory</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1847 onward</td><td>irrigation canals dug from mountain streams; land assigned by the church rather than claimed individually</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1848</td><td>the Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo puts the valley inside the United States after all</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1850 to 1896</td><td>Utah is a territory; statehood comes only after the church ends the practice of plural marriage</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("map",74)}${ico("mountain",74)}${ico("wateringCan",74)}</div>`,qs:["They chose a dry valley a thousand miles from anyone. Given what had happened in Ohio, Missouri and Illinois, why would that be the point?","Land was assigned by the church and water was managed collectively rather than claimed by whoever got there first. Why would that be necessary in the Great Basin?","In 1846 they left the United States and in 1848 they were inside it again. What problem did that create, and how was it eventually settled?"],key:["Isolation was the goal. They had been expelled from three states by neighbors and state authorities, so the value of the Great Basin was that nobody else wanted it and no state government was there. Distance was protection, and they expected to govern themselves outside American jurisdiction. Partial: calling the basin a poor choice misses the point that nobody else wanted it, and is approaching. (HSS-8.8)","Because farming there is impossible without irrigation, and irrigation requires canals that no single family can build or maintain and a rule for sharing a limited stream. Central assignment of land and collective control of water made settlement work in a place where the usual American pattern of individual claims would have produced ruin and conflict over the first dry summer. Partial: naming the irrigation with nothing said about why no family could do it alone is approaching. (HSS-8.8)","It put a community that had left to escape American authority back under it, and the conflict that followed ran for decades over who governed the territory, over federal officials, and over plural marriage, which Congress legislated against repeatedly. It was settled when the church announced an end to the practice in 1890 and Utah was admitted as a state in 1896. Partial: naming the conflict with nothing said about how it was settled is approaching. (HSS-8.8)"]},{std:["HSS-8.9"],n:4,dur:110,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing antislavery politics, popular sovereignty and the difference between restriction and abolition",html:()=>`<p class="big">Abolitionists argued about whether to enter politics at all. Those who did built three parties in fourteen years.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Party</th><th>Founded</th><th>What it demanded</th><th>Best result</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Liberty</td><td>1840</td><td>abolition, on the ground that slavery was a sin</td><td>about 2 percent of the vote in 1844</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Free Soil</td><td>1848</td><td>no slavery in the territories; said nothing about the existing states</td><td>about 10 percent in 1848</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Republican</td><td>1854</td><td>no slavery in the territories</td><td>the presidency in 1860</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">In 1858 Abraham Lincoln and Stephen Douglas ran against each other for an Illinois Senate seat and held seven debates in front of enormous crowds. Douglas argued for popular sovereignty, that the settlers of each territory should vote slavery up or down and that the question was none of the nation's business. Lincoln answered that a nation founded on the proposition that all men are created equal could not treat slavery as a matter of local preference. Douglas won the seat. Lincoln won the argument's audience and the presidency two years later.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("flag",74)}${ico("sprout",74)}${ico("hands",74)}</div>`,qs:["The party that demanded the most got the fewest votes and the party that demanded less won. What does that pattern show about how the change actually happened?","Popular sovereignty sounds democratic: let the people there decide. State Lincoln's objection to it, and then state the strongest reply Douglas could make.","Garrison refused to vote at all and called the Constitution a proslavery document. Explain his reasoning, and then make the case against him.","If the Republicans only promised to stop slavery spreading, why did slaveholders treat their victory as an emergency?"],key:["That a movement can shift the whole field without winning on its own terms. The Liberty party's full demand was unacceptable to most Northern voters, but restricting slavery's spread could be argued on free labor and free land grounds that appealed to people who were not abolitionists. The narrower demand carried a national election, and the war then produced the broader result. Partial: noting the vote totals with nothing said about the narrower demand carrying the election is approaching. (HSS-8.9)","Lincoln's objection: a vote cannot make it right, because the people voting are not the people whose freedom is being voted on, and a country whose founding claim is that all men are created equal cannot leave that claim to local majorities without abandoning it. Douglas's strongest reply: self government means the settlers of a territory decide their own institutions, as every state had, and forcing a national answer on them is exactly the outside rule the Revolution was fought against. A good answer gives both with force rather than knocking down a weak version. Partial: one side's case given, or the other side's case given in a weak version, is approaching. (HSS-8.9)","He reasoned that the Constitution counted enslaved people for representation, obliged the return of people who escaped, and protected the trade until 1808, so voting under it meant swearing to uphold a proslavery compact. Against him: refusing to vote leaves the decisions to slavery's defenders, and in fact political antislavery took the presidency in twenty years and the Thirteenth Amendment followed, which moral suasion alone had not achieved. Partial: one of his reasoning and the case against him is approaching. (HSS-8.9)","Because stopping the spread meant a growing majority of free states in Congress and in the electoral college, and eventually the votes to amend the Constitution, so a promise about territories was understood as the slow end of the system. They also noted that Lincoln had carried no Southern state, which told them the section could no longer protect slavery from inside the Union. Partial: saying they disliked him with no account of votes in Congress or the electoral college is approaching. (HSS-8.9)"]}]},{id:25,title:"Divided and Rebuilt",short:"War and After",parts:[{std:["HSS-8.10"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the border states and divided communities during the Civil War",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four slave states never seceded.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>State</th><th>Why it mattered</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Maryland</td><td>surrounds Washington on three sides; the capital's rail link runs through it</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Kentucky</td><td>the Ohio River line and the routes into Tennessee</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Missouri</td><td>St. Louis, the Missouri River, and the approach to the Mississippi</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Delaware</td><td>small, but adjacent to Philadelphia</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">In 1861 the counties of western Virginia refused to leave the Union with the rest of the state, formed their own government, and in 1863 entered the Union as West Virginia. In Kentucky and Missouri, brothers and neighbors enlisted on opposite sides.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("map",74)}${ico("flag",74)}${ico("bridge",74)}</div>`,qs:["Explain why Lincoln took such care with Maryland and Kentucky, even at the cost of looking slow on slavery.","The western Virginia counties left Virginia using the same argument Virginia used to leave the Union. Is that consistent? Defend your answer.","Name one thing the existence of these states tells you about the claim that the war was North against South."],key:["Losing Maryland would have cut Washington off and surrounded the capital with hostile territory, and losing Kentucky would have moved the front to the Ohio River and handed the Confederacy the river line and a route north. Both were slave states whose loyalty depended on the war not being presented as a war against slavery, which is a large part of why the 1861 war aim was the Union alone. Partial: one of the two states explained is approaching. (HSS-8.10)","Any defended answer. For consistency: both claimed a people may govern themselves rather than be governed by a majority they reject, so the western counties applied the principle to themselves. Against: Virginia's secessionists denied that any part of their state could do to them what they were doing to the Union, which shows the principle was being used rather than believed. The reasoning is what is rated. Partial: a verdict with no reasoning is approaching. (HSS-8.10)","That the division was not a clean line between two regions. Four slave states stayed, one state split in two, and in Kentucky and Missouri the war ran through families and counties. The real division was over slavery and the Union, and it cut across geography wherever the economy and the population did not match the secessionists' map. Partial: describing a clean line between two regions is the misreading to watch for and is approaching. (HSS-8.10)"]},{std:["HSS-8.11"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the conflict between Congress and President Johnson over Reconstruction",html:()=>`<p class="big">Andrew Johnson, a Tennessee Unionist, became president in April 1865. His plan readmitted Southern states quickly, pardoned most former Confederates, and left the new state governments to decide what rights freedpeople would have. Those governments passed the Black Codes and sent former Confederate officials to Congress. Congress refused to seat them, passed the Civil Rights Act of 1866 over Johnson's veto, wrote the Fourteenth Amendment, and in 1867 put the South under military districts until new constitutions were written. Johnson fought it at every step, and in 1868 the House impeached him. The Senate fell one vote short of removing him.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("books",74)}${ico("flag",74)}${ico("hands",74)}</div>`,qs:["Johnson and Congress agreed the war had been won. What exactly were they disagreeing about?","Congress refused to seat the men the Southern states elected. On what authority, and why do it?","The Senate kept Johnson in office by one vote. Argue whether that outcome mattered."],key:["About what victory had settled. Johnson held that the war had settled secession only, so the states returned as they were minus slavery, with their own governments deciding the rest. Congress held that it had also settled the standing of four million freedpeople, which required federal guarantees the former Confederate leadership would never provide. It is a disagreement about the war's purpose, carried on after the fighting. Partial: one side's position given is approaching. (HSS-8.11)","On each chamber's constitutional authority to judge the qualifications of its own members. They did it because seating former Confederate officials would have handed the writing of Reconstruction to the people it was meant to restrain, and because the Three Fifths Compromise was gone, so Southern states now had more seats than before the war while denying freedpeople the vote. Partial: one of the authority and the reason given is approaching. (HSS-8.11)","Any argued answer. That it mattered: Johnson stayed and kept obstructing, so Congress had to legislate around him and much of Reconstruction depended on Grant's election later that year. That it mattered less: Congress already had the votes to override him and had taken control, and the vote may have spared the office from becoming removable over policy disagreement. A good answer takes a position and gives a reason. Partial: a position with no reason is approaching. (HSS-8.11)"]},{std:["HSS-8.12"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing new inventions and tracing their economic effects",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four inventions and the years they came into use.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Invention</th><th>Year</th></tr>
     <tr><td>refrigerated railroad car</td><td>1870s</td></tr>
     <tr><td>telephone</td><td>1876</td></tr>
     <tr><td>practical electric light</td><td>1879</td></tr>
     <tr><td>typewriter in offices</td><td>1870s and 1880s</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("lamp",74)}${ico("phone",74)}${ico("truck",74)}</div>`,qs:["Trace the refrigerated car's effect from a ranch in Texas to a kitchen in Boston.","Electric light let a factory run after dark. Name one gain and one cost of that.","The typewriter is usually listed as an office machine. It also changed who worked in offices. Explain."],key:["Cattle could be slaughtered near the rail hub in Chicago and the meat shipped east already cut, instead of shipping live animals that lost weight and died on the way. That made beef cheaper and available year round in Eastern cities, concentrated meatpacking into a few enormous firms, and gave ranchers a reliable national buyer. One car design reorganized an entire industry. Partial: naming the refrigerated car with nothing said about how it reorganized the industry is approaching. (HSS-8.12)","Gain: expensive machinery and buildings could be used around the clock, which lowered the cost of each item made, and work no longer stopped with the daylight in winter. Cost: the working day and the night shift were set by the employer rather than by the sun, which lengthened hours and broke up sleep and family time. The same technology raised output and extended control over workers' lives. Partial: one of the gain and the cost given is approaching. (HSS-8.12)","Typing was a new job with no established trade or apprenticeship attached, so employers filled it with women at lower pay than male clerks, and schools opened to train them. Within two decades office work shifted from almost entirely male to substantially female, which gave many women their first respectable wage work outside teaching and also set up a lower paid track. Partial: noting the new jobs with nothing said about the lower paid track is approaching. (HSS-8.12)"]}]},{id:26,title:"What Tells a Tree to Bloom",short:"Heredity",parts:[{std:["LS3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"tracing a change in a gene through the protein it codes for to the trait that results",html:()=>`<p class="big">The wild cherries by the mill tree carry four versions of one gene. It codes for a protein that measures day length and, once the days are long enough, signals the buds to open.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Gene version</th><th>Protein the gene codes for</th><th>When the tree blooms</th></tr>
     <tr><td>P1, unchanged</td><td>full length sensor, signals at 13 hours of daylight</td><td>first week of April</td></tr>
     <tr><td>P2, one base changed in the light absorbing pocket</td><td>full length sensor that needs 15 hours before it signals</td><td>third week of May</td></tr>
     <tr><td>P3, the last third of the gene deleted</td><td>sensor cannot grip its partner protein, so it never passes the signal on</td><td>only after a hard cold shock, often not at all</td></tr>
     <tr><td>P4, the whole gene duplicated, two working copies</td><td>twice as much of the same sensor, signals at 12 hours</td><td>last week of March</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("cherryBlossom",74)}${ico("sun",74)}</div>`,qs:["Follow P3 from the missing stretch of DNA to a tree that may never bloom, naming each step.","P2's sensor is complete and present in the normal amount. Say why it blooms six weeks late, and what that shows about where in a protein a change falls.","P4 changed not one amino acid. Explain how it moved the bloom earlier."],key:["The last third of the gene is gone, so the protein that is built is missing the end that grips its partner. The sensor can still absorb light and can still tell that the day is long enough, but it has no way to hand the signal on, so the buds are never told to open. Missing DNA, shortened protein, lost gripping surface, broken signal, no bloom unless a different route such as a cold shock opens the buds instead. Partial: a chain that jumps from the missing DNA to the tree not blooming with the lost gripping surface left out is approaching. (LS3)","The change sits in the pocket that absorbs the light, so the sensor needs a stronger signal, meaning a longer day, before it switches on. The protein is the right length and present in the right amount, and it still works, just at a different setting, which is why the bloom is six weeks late rather than absent. Where inside a protein a change lands decides what it costs: a change in the working pocket shifts the threshold, while the same change in an idle loop would shift nothing. Partial: saying the sensor needs a longer day, with nothing said about where inside the protein the change fell mattering, is approaching. (LS3)","Two copies of the gene are read, so the cell builds twice as much of the identical sensor. With twice as many sensors, enough of them are switched on by a shorter day to pass the signal, so the tree clears its threshold at 12 hours instead of 13 and blooms a week or two earlier. The amount of a protein is as much a part of the trait as the protein's sequence is. Partial: saying there are two copies of the gene, with nothing said about more sensors clearing the threshold on a shorter day, is approaching. (LS3)"]}]},{id:27,title:"Eyes and the Creek Bank",short:"Evolution",parts:[{std:["LS4"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study both charts. Answer out loud.",desc:"telling inherited similarity from similar function and reading a fossil sequence",html:()=>`<p class="big">The creek bank below the mill, recorded layer by layer. Layers pile up newest on top.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Rock</th><th>Fossils in it</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1, 0 to 2 m</td><td>silt</td><td>clams and mayfly wings like those alive today</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2, 2 to 7 m</td><td>sandstone</td><td>insects with folding wings, a bee with pollen baskets on its legs, and the pollen of flowering plants</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3, 7 to 16 m</td><td>shale</td><td>winged insects whose wings cannot fold, no bees, and no flowering plant pollen</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4, 16 to 28 m</td><td>limestone</td><td>six legged animals with no wings at all</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Two eyes that both form a focused image.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Feature</th><th>vertebrate eye, as in a trout or a bird</th><th>octopus eye</th></tr>
     <tr><td>lens</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>light sensing layer at the back</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>nerve fibres run</td><td>across the front of that layer</td><td>behind that layer</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the layer develops from</td><td>an outgrowth of the brain</td><td>a fold of the skin</td></tr>
     <tr><td>blind spot where the nerve leaves</td><td>yes</td><td>no</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("bee",74)}${ico("butterfly",74)}${ico("octopus",74)}</div>`,qs:["Read the creek bank layers from oldest to youngest and describe the sequence, including what turns up with the flowering plant pollen.","Both eyes make images. Say whether the chart points to a close relationship, and name the two rows that decide it.","A trout embryo and a chicken embryo both grow the light sensing layer as an outgrowth of the brain, and an octopus embryo never does. Say what that adds."],key:["Oldest first: layer 4, six legged animals with no wings; layer 3, winged insects whose wings cannot fold, with no bees and no flowering plant pollen; layer 2, insects with folding wings, a bee carrying pollen baskets, and flowering plant pollen; layer 1, the living forms. Wings appear, then the hinge that lets them fold, and the bee with pollen baskets shows up in the same layer as the first flowering plants and in no layer below it. Structures appear in order, and a structure built for flowers does not appear before flowers do. Partial: the four layers in the right order with nothing said about the pollen basket bee appearing in no layer below the first flowers is approaching. (LS4)","No, it points away from a close relationship. The two eyes agree on the parts a working eye needs, a lens and a light sensing layer, and disagree on everything about how they are put together. The deciding rows are the one that says where the nerve fibres run, in front in one and behind in the other, and the one that says where the light sensing layer comes from, the brain in one and the skin in the other. Those are differences in the underlying plan, which is what ancestry shows up in, while the lens is a requirement of focusing light that any image forming eye has to meet. Partial: answering that the eyes are not closely related with only one of the two deciding rows named is approaching. (LS4)","It shows the vertebrate plan is laid down by inherited instructions that the octopus has never had, so the resemblance between the two adult eyes is not inherited at all. Two separate lines arrived at a similar answer to the same problem, which is why their shared features are the ones physics demands and their differences are the ones development dictates. Development is the test that tells an inherited similarity from a similar solution, and the creek bank's fossils put the whole sequence in time. Partial: saying the octopus never follows those instructions, with nothing said about development being the test that tells an inherited similarity from a similar solution, is approaching. (LS4)"]},{std:["LS4"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study both sets of numbers. Work on your paper and answer out loud.",desc:"explaining natural selection from a trait distribution and distinguishing it from artificial selection",html:()=>`<p class="big">The mill pond was dredged and its water became clear and open, with little cover left. Sticklebacks live in it, and the bony plates along their sides make a fish harder for a heron to swallow. One hundred and eighty fish were scored each generation.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Bony side plates</th><th>Generation 1</th><th>Generation 6</th><th>Generation 14</th></tr>
     <tr><td>fewer than 10</td><td>94</td><td>51</td><td>17</td></tr>
     <tr><td>10 to 20</td><td>61</td><td>79</td><td>66</td></tr>
     <tr><td>more than 20</td><td>25</td><td>50</td><td>97</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The mill's own wheat is a different story. For seventy years the miller kept seed only from the heads that held their grain until threshing. Grain lost in the field before threshing fell from 22 percent to 3 percent.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("fish",74)}${ico("wheat",74)}${ico("bluebird",74)}</div>`,qs:["Explain the shift in the plate counts. Name the variation, what is doing the selecting, and what has to be inherited.","Work out the shares with more than 20 plates in generation 1 and in generation 14, then say what the chart would look like if plate number were not inherited.","Compare the sticklebacks with the mill's wheat, and say which of the two changes could be reversed faster and why."],key:["Plate number already varies among the sticklebacks and is inherited. Clear open water means a heron sees more fish, and a fish with many plates is harder to swallow and more likely to be let go, so heavily plated fish survive to spawn more often than lightly plated ones. Their offspring inherit the gene versions for more plates, so the distribution shifts from 94 of 180 with fewer than ten plates to 17. The heron does not add plates to any fish; it changes which fish spawn. Partial: two of the three steps given is approaching, and the inheritance step is usually the one left out. (LS4)","Generation 1: 25 of 180, which is about 14 percent. Generation 14: 97 of 180, which is about 54 percent. If plate number were not inherited, each generation's offspring would start from the same spread as before no matter which parents survived, so the three rows would stay near 94, 61 and 25 forever. A flat chart under strong selection is exactly what a trait that is not passed on looks like. Partial: working out the 14 and 54 percent with no account of what a chart for a trait that is not inherited would look like is approaching. (LS4)","The pond, because the selection there comes from the conditions. Fill the pond with weed and cover again and herons take fewer fish, so the pressure on plate number drops, and because many plates cost energy to build the distribution could drift back within a few generations. The wheat would be far slower to reverse: the miller selected one trait for seventy years and the gene versions for heads that shatter early have largely been bred out, so there is little variation left to select back, and what has been lost has to arrive again by new mutation. Losing variation is what makes a change hard to undo. Partial: choosing the pond with nothing said about the wheat having lost the variation that would be selected back is approaching. (LS4)"]}]},{id:28,title:"The Shadow of the Mill Post",short:"Universe",parts:[{std:["MS-ESS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the chart. Work on your paper and answer out loud.",desc:"explaining the yearly cycle from shadow measurements and naming gravity's part in orbits",html:()=>`<p class="big">A post exactly 2 m tall stands in the mill yard. Its shadow was measured at noon on five dates.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Date</th><th>Length of the noon shadow</th></tr>
     <tr><td>March 20</td><td>2.00 m</td></tr>
     <tr><td>May 5</td><td>1.10 m</td></tr>
     <tr><td>June 21</td><td>0.79 m</td></tr>
     <tr><td>August 7</td><td>1.09 m</td></tr>
     <tr><td>September 22</td><td>2.01 m</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("sun",74)}${ico("tree",74)}${ico("ruler",74)}</div>`,qs:["Describe the pattern in the shadow lengths and explain why the readings are almost symmetric about June 21.","On March 20 the shadow is exactly as long as the post is tall. Work out the sun's height above the horizon that day and say what it tells you about where the mill is.","Name the force that keeps Earth on the path producing this pattern, and say what the pattern would look like if Earth's axis were not tilted."],key:["The shadow shortens from 2.00 m in March to 0.79 m on June 21 and then lengthens again to 2.01 m in September, so the noon sun climbs to a peak in June and falls back. The readings are symmetric because the shadow depends on how high the noon sun is, and that height depends on where Earth is along its orbit relative to the tilt of its axis. May 5 and August 7 sit about the same number of days either side of June 21, so the tilt presents the sun at almost the same height on both, 1.10 m and 1.09 m. Partial: describing the shortening and lengthening with nothing said about May 5 and August 7 sitting the same distance either side of June 21 is approaching. (MS-ESS1)","If the shadow equals the height of the post, the sun is at 45 degrees above the horizon, because the shadow and the post form two equal sides of a right angled triangle. March 20 is an equinox, and at an equinox the noon sun's height equals 90 degrees minus the latitude, so the latitude is 90 minus 45, which is 45 degrees north. Partial: getting the 45 degrees with the latitude not worked out from it is approaching. (MS-ESS1)","Gravity, with its strength set by the sun's mass, Earth's mass and the distance between them. With no tilt, the noon sun would reach the same height every day of the year, so the shadow would be the same length in March, June and December, there would be no seasons anywhere, and the day would be about twelve hours long everywhere all year. The pattern in the chart exists only because the axis is tilted. Partial: naming gravity with no prediction made about what an untilted Earth would show is approaching. (MS-ESS1)"]},{std:["MS-ESS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Use both charts. Work on your paper and tell your teacher each answer.",desc:"comparing look back times across the universe and bracketing an intrusion between dated layers",html:()=>`<p class="big">Distances given as how long the light has been travelling.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Object</th><th>Light travel time</th></tr>
     <tr><td>the sun</td><td>8.3 minutes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Sirius</td><td>8.6 years</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the Orion Nebula</td><td>1,340 years</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the Andromeda galaxy</td><td>2,500,000 years</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The creek bank again, dated. A granite sheet cuts up through the limestone and the shale and stops at the base of the sandstone.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Unit, top to bottom</th><th>Age of the rock</th></tr>
     <tr><td>silt</td><td>5,000 years</td></tr>
     <tr><td>sandstone</td><td>40 million years</td></tr>
     <tr><td>shale, cut by the granite sheet</td><td>200 million years</td></tr>
     <tr><td>limestone, cut by the granite sheet</td><td>310 million years</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The granite sheet itself is dated at 160 million years.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("star",74)}${ico("telescope",74)}${ico("mountain",74)}</div>`,qs:["Explain in your own words why a telescope is a way of looking into the past, and work out how much further back the Orion Nebula's light reaches than Sirius's.","A model of the solar system runs along the mill lane with the sun at the gate and Neptune, at 30.1 AU, 90 m away. Say where Earth goes and why Sirius cannot be put on the lane at all.","Use the layers alone, without the granite's date, to bracket when the sheet formed, then check your bracket against the 160 million year figure."],key:["Light takes time to travel, so what arrives now left its source a while ago and shows that source as it was then, not as it is now. The sun is seen 8.3 minutes late, Sirius 8.6 years late, the Orion Nebula 1,340 years late. The nebula's light reaches 1,340 divided by 8.6, about 156 times further back than Sirius's. Looking further out is the same thing as looking further back. Partial: explaining why a telescope looks back in time with the 156 times not worked out is approaching. (MS-ESS1)","Neptune at 30.1 AU stands at 90 m, so 1 AU is 90 divided by 30.1, which is about 2.99 m, and Earth goes at about 3.0 m from the gate. Sirius is 8.6 light years away, which is about 544,000 AU, so on this scale it would be 544,000 times 2.99 m, over 1,600 km from the gate. A model that fits the whole solar system in a lane cannot hold even the second nearest star. Partial: placing Earth at 3.0 m with no figure worked out for Sirius is approaching. (MS-ESS1)","The sheet cuts the limestone and the shale, so it formed after both of those were already there, which puts it younger than 200 million years. It stops at the base of the sandstone, so the sandstone was laid down over it afterward, which puts it older than 40 million years. The bracket is between 200 and 40 million years, and the measured 160 million years falls inside it. Cross cutting relationships give the order and the dates give the numbers, and the two check each other. Partial: one end of the bracket argued from the layers is approaching, and the sandstone above is usually the one left out. (MS-ESS1)"]}]},{id:29,title:"Nitrogen in the Valley",short:"Human Impact",parts:[{std:["MS-ESS3"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Work on your paper and answer out loud.",desc:"separating population growth from per person consumption in total demand on a resource",html:()=>`<p class="big">Nitrogen fertilizer used on the valley's farms. About a tenth of what is applied ends up in the mill creek, and the creek can take about 180 tonnes a year before the pond blooms with algae every summer.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Year</th><th>People fed from the valley</th><th>kg of nitrogen per person per year</th><th>Total applied</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1965</td><td>60,000</td><td>11</td><td>660 tonnes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1985</td><td>95,000</td><td>19</td><td>1,805 tonnes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2005</td><td>140,000</td><td>17</td><td>2,380 tonnes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2025</td><td>180,000</td><td>14</td><td>2,520 tonnes</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("wheat",74)}${ico("tractor",74)}${ico("drop",74)}</div>`,qs:["The total almost quadrupled from 1965 to 2025 while the per person figure fell after 1985. Separate the two causes with numbers.","Work out how much nitrogen reaches the creek in 2025, how far over the limit that is, and the per person figure that would end it.","Name two effects on Earth's systems when more nitrogen reaches the water than the creek can take, and say which factor in the chart the valley can act on."],key:["People trebled, from 60,000 to 180,000, a factor of 3. Per person use rose from 11 kg to 14 kg, a factor of about 1.27. Multiplied together, 3 times 1.27 is about 3.8, and 660 times 3.8 is about 2,520 tonnes, which matches. Population growth did nearly all of it; per person use rose until 1985 and has been falling since, which has only slowed the climb rather than reversed it. Partial: naming both factors with only one of the two multipliers worked out is approaching. (MS-ESS3)","A tenth of 2,520 tonnes is 252 tonnes reaching the creek, against a limit of 180, so the valley is 72 tonnes over. To get the creek down to 180 tonnes, the total applied would have to come down to 1,800 tonnes, and among 180,000 people that is 1,800,000 divided by 180,000, which is 10 kg each. That is below even the 11 kg of 1965, while feeding three times as many people. Partial: getting the 252 tonnes and the 72 over with the 10 kg figure not worked out is approaching. (MS-ESS3)","Any two: nitrogen in the water feeds algae, which bloom and then die and rot, and the rotting uses up the oxygen so fish and insects suffocate; the shaded water below a bloom loses its plants; nitrogen that sinks into groundwater makes well water unsafe to drink; and the nitrogen that leaves the soil as gas adds to the gases warming the atmosphere. The valley can act on per person use, through better timing, soil testing, cover crops and buffer strips along the creek, since nobody at the farm bureau decides how many people the valley feeds. Partial: two effects named with nothing said about per person use being the factor the valley can act on is approaching. (MS-ESS3)"]}]},{id:30,title:"Off the Raft",short:"Forces",parts:[{std:["MS-PS2"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Use both charts. Work on your paper and answer out loud.",desc:"applying the third law to a push between two objects and relating force and mass to motion",html:()=>`<p class="big">A crew member steps off a floating raft onto the bank of the mill pond.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Object</th><th>Mass</th><th>Speed just after the step</th></tr>
     <tr><td>crew member</td><td>55 kg</td><td>1.1 m/s forward</td></tr>
     <tr><td>raft</td><td>110 kg</td><td>0.55 m/s backward</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">A seed crate is then pushed across the mill floor with a measured force.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Trial</th><th>Force</th><th>Mass</th><th>Acceleration</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>50 N</td><td>25 kg</td><td>2 m/s each second</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>50 N</td><td>10 kg</td><td>5 m/s each second</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>200 N</td><td>25 kg</td><td>8 m/s each second</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>126 N</td><td>42 kg</td><td>3 m/s each second</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("canoe",74)}${ico("box",74)}${ico("drop",74)}</div>`,qs:["The crew member pushed back on the raft without meaning to. Explain with Newton's third law, and compare the sizes of the two forces.","Multiply mass by speed for each row of the first chart. Say what the two products show, then work out the raft's speed if the crew member had massed 70 kg and still left at 1.1 m/s.","Which two trials differ only in mass and which only in force? Say what each pair shows, then verify trial 4 with the rule."],key:["To step forward the crew member pushes backward on the raft, and the raft pushes forward on the crew member with a force equal in size and opposite in direction, at the same instant. The forward push on the person is what moves them, so the step and the raft drifting back are the same single event seen from the two ends of one pair of forces. Nothing can push you forward without being pushed backward. Partial: saying the raft was pushed back, with nothing said about the forward push on the person being what moves them, is approaching. (MS-PS2)","Crew member: 55 times 1.1 is 60.5. Raft: 110 times 0.55 is also 60.5. Equal in size and opposite in direction, which is what a pair of equal and opposite forces acting for the same time produces. A 70 kg person at 1.1 m/s carries 77, so the 110 kg raft would move at 77 divided by 110, which is 0.7 m/s backward. Partial: finding both products equal to 60.5 with the 0.7 m/s prediction not worked out is approaching. (MS-PS2)","Trials 1 and 2 differ only in mass, 25 kg against 10 kg at 50 N, and the acceleration rises from 2 to 5, so less mass means more acceleration for the same force. Trials 1 and 3 differ only in force, 50 N against 200 N at 25 kg, and the acceleration rises from 2 to 8, so four times the force gives four times the acceleration. Trial 4 verifies: 42 kg times 3 m/s each second is 126 N. Partial: naming both pairs of trials with trial 4 not checked against force equals mass times acceleration is approaching. (MS-PS2)"]},{std:["MS-PS2"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Use all three charts. Answer out loud.",desc:"identifying what changes electric and magnetic forces and relating weight to mass and radius",html:()=>`<p class="big">The mill's grain gate is held shut by an electromagnet.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Trial</th><th>Turns of wire</th><th>Current</th><th>Core</th><th>Holding force</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>120</td><td>1 A</td><td>iron</td><td>11 N</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>120</td><td>3 A</td><td>iron</td><td>33 N</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>360</td><td>1 A</td><td>iron</td><td>33 N</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>120</td><td>1 A</td><td>aluminium</td><td>1 N</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">A charged balloon is held beside a thin falling stream of dry sand and the sideways pull is measured.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Balloon</th><th>Bend at 2 cm</th><th>Bend at 4 cm</th></tr>
     <tr><td>rubbed 4 times</td><td>3.0 mm</td><td>0.8 mm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>rubbed 16 times</td><td>11.0 mm</td><td>2.8 mm</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The same 6 kg sack of seed, weighed in three places.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Where</th><th>Weight on the scale</th></tr>
     <tr><td>the mill yard</td><td>58.8 N</td></tr>
     <tr><td>on the moon</td><td>9.7 N</td></tr>
     <tr><td>on a world with four times Earth's mass and twice Earth's radius</td><td>58.8 N</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("key",74)}${ico("balloons",74)}${ico("globe",74)}</div>`,qs:["Use the first chart. Name the three factors and the pair that shows each, and say which one no amount of extra current could make up for.","Use the second chart. Describe both patterns, then say which of the two factors would be easier to control in a mill full of dust.","Explain all three seed sack weights, and say why the third world gives exactly the same reading as the mill yard."],key:["Current: trials 1 and 2, where trebling it from 1 A to 3 A trebles the hold from 11 N to 33 N. Turns of wire: trials 1 and 3, where trebling them from 120 to 360 also trebles the hold from 11 N to 33 N. The core: trials 1 and 4, where swapping iron for aluminium drops the hold from 11 N to 1 N. The core is the one extra current cannot make up for, because aluminium is not magnetic and so adds almost nothing to the field no matter how much current flows; iron concentrates the field, and nothing else in the chart substitutes for that. Partial: all three factors named with trial pairs but nothing said about why current cannot make up for an aluminium core is approaching. (MS-PS2)","Charge: at a fixed distance, four times the rubbing roughly trebles to quadruples the bend, 3.0 mm to 11.0 mm at 2 cm and 0.8 mm to 2.8 mm at 4 cm. Distance: at a fixed charge, doubling the distance cuts the bend to about a quarter, 3.0 mm to 0.8 mm and 11.0 mm to 2.8 mm. Distance would be easier to control in a dusty mill, because it is set by where you mount the thing and stays put, while the charge on a surface leaks away quickly once dust and damp settle on it. Partial: describing both patterns with no judgment given on which factor is easier to control in a dusty mill is approaching. (MS-PS2)","The sack holds the same 6 kg of matter in all three places. Weight is the gravitational force on it, which grows with the mass of the world and falls off with the square of the distance to its center. The moon's small mass gives 9.7 N. The third world has four times Earth's mass, which would multiply the pull by four, and twice Earth's radius, which divides it by two squared, that is by four. Four divided by four is one, so the two changes cancel exactly and the scale reads the same 58.8 N. Partial: explaining the moon reading with nothing said about the third world's four times mass and twice radius cancelling is approaching. (MS-PS2)"]}]},{id:31,title:"The Sluice Gate",short:"Energy",parts:[{std:["MS-PS3"],n:4,dur:110,dir:"Use both charts. Work on your paper and tell your teacher each answer.",desc:"relating kinetic energy to mass and speed and stored energy to the arrangement of objects",html:()=>`<p class="big">A loaded barrow's kinetic energy was measured on the mill floor.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Trial</th><th>Mass</th><th>Speed</th><th>Kinetic energy</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>6 kg</td><td>4 m/s</td><td>48 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>6 kg</td><td>8 m/s</td><td>192 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>24 kg</td><td>4 m/s</td><td>192 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>6 kg</td><td>2 m/s</td><td>12 J</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Stored energy measured for the mill's 500 kg sluice gate raised to three heights, and for the spring in the seed drill.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Arrangement</th><th>Stored energy</th></tr>
     <tr><td>sluice gate raised 0.2 m</td><td>980 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>sluice gate raised 0.5 m</td><td>2,450 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>sluice gate raised 1.0 m</td><td>4,900 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>seed drill spring pulled 6 cm</td><td>0.9 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>seed drill spring pulled 18 cm</td><td>8.1 J</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("drop",74)}${ico("box",74)}</div>`,qs:["Trials 2 and 3 both come to 192 J. Say what each one changed from trial 1 and by how much.","Use trials 1 and 4 to state the rule for speed, then work out the energy of a 24 kg load moving at 8 m/s.","Use the sluice gate rows. State the rule, and explain why the gate's stored energy has no single correct value.","Say which store rises in step with its distance and which rises with the square of it, then explain what about the force makes the difference."],key:["Trial 2 doubled trial 1's speed, from 4 m/s to 8 m/s, and the energy went up four times, 48 J to 192 J. Trial 3 quadrupled trial 1's mass, from 6 kg to 24 kg, and the energy also went up four times. So doubling the speed does the same work as quadrupling the mass, because speed is squared in the energy and mass is not. Partial: naming both changes with nothing said about speed being squared while mass is not is approaching. (MS-PS3)","Trial 4 halves trial 1's speed and the energy drops to a quarter, 48 J to 12 J, so kinetic energy goes up with the square of the speed. A 24 kg load at 8 m/s is four times trial 1's mass and twice its speed, so the energy is 48 times 4 times 4, which is 768 J. Partial: stating the squared rule with the 768 J not worked out, or a figure of 384 J that scales the speed once rather than squaring it, is approaching. (MS-PS3)","Stored energy is proportional to the height raised: 0.2 m gives 980 J, two and a half times that height gives two and a half times the energy at 2,450 J, and five times gives 4,900 J. The figures are measured from the gate's closed position, which somebody chose. Measured from the stream bed below, or from the top of its frame, the same gate at the same height would be given a different number. Stored energy belongs to the arrangement of the gate and Earth, so the reference position is part of the answer and not an afterthought. Partial: stating the proportion to height with nothing said about the closed position being a chosen reference is approaching. (MS-PS3)","The sluice gate rises in step with its height. The spring rises with the square of its stretch, 0.9 J at 6 cm and 8.1 J, nine times as much, at 18 cm, which is three times the stretch. Earth pulls the gate with practically the same force at every height, so each extra centimetre of lift stores the same amount. The spring pulls back harder the further it is stretched, so each extra centimetre stores more than the last. The rule follows how the force itself changes as the parts move. Partial: naming which store follows which rule with nothing said about the spring pulling back harder the further it is stretched is approaching. (MS-PS3)"]}]},{id:32,title:"Waves in the Mill Race",short:"Waves",parts:[{std:["MS-PS4"],n:3,dur:80,dir:"Look at the chart. Work on your paper and answer out loud.",desc:"relating amplitude to the energy in a wave and using wavelength and frequency to find wave speed",html:()=>`<p class="big">Waves were sent down the mill race and a hinged board at the far end measured the energy each crest delivered.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Wave</th><th>Amplitude</th><th>Wavelength</th><th>Crests per second</th><th>Energy each crest delivers</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A</td><td>1.5 cm</td><td>45 cm</td><td>4</td><td>1 unit</td></tr>
     <tr><td>B</td><td>3.0 cm</td><td>45 cm</td><td>4</td><td>4 units</td></tr>
     <tr><td>C</td><td>4.5 cm</td><td>45 cm</td><td>4</td><td>9 units</td></tr>
     <tr><td>D</td><td>4.5 cm</td><td>90 cm</td><td>2</td><td>9 units</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("wave",74)}${ico("drop",74)}</div>`,qs:["Waves A, B and C differ only in amplitude. State the relationship between amplitude and the energy a crest delivers.","Work out the speed of each of waves C and D, and say what that tells you about what sets the speed of a water wave in this race.","Wave D delivers the same energy per crest as wave C but half as many crests each second. Say what that means for the energy arriving at the board over a minute."],key:["Doubling the amplitude from 1.5 cm to 3.0 cm multiplied the energy by four, 1 unit to 4 units, and trebling it to 4.5 cm multiplied it by nine, 1 unit to 9 units. The energy a crest delivers goes up with the square of the amplitude, so amplitude is the measure of the energy in a wave. Partial: saying a bigger wave carries more energy, with nothing said about the energy going up with the square of the amplitude, is approaching. (MS-PS4)","Speed is wavelength times crests per second. C: 0.45 m times 4 is 1.8 m per second. D: 0.90 m times 2 is also 1.8 m per second. Both waves run at the same speed although their wavelengths and frequencies differ, so the speed here is set by the water and the race, not by how the waves were made. That is why halving the frequency doubled the wavelength. Partial: working out one of the two speeds, or both with nothing said about the water and the race setting the speed, is approaching. (MS-PS4)","Over a minute, wave C delivers 4 crests a second times 60 seconds, which is 240 crests at 9 units each, so 2,160 units. Wave D delivers 2 crests a second times 60, which is 120 crests at 9 units each, so 1,080 units, half as much. Energy per crest depends only on amplitude, but the energy arriving over a stretch of time depends on how many crests arrive, so frequency matters for the total even though it does not change what one crest carries. Partial: saying wave D delivers less over a minute with the 2,160 and 1,080 units not worked out is approaching. (MS-PS4)"]},{std:["MS-PS4"],n:3,dur:80,dir:"Use the chart and the two methods. Answer out loud.",desc:"reading reflection, absorption and transmission from data and comparing a digital with an analog signal",html:()=>`<p class="big">A lamp sends 100 units of light at each material. One meter sits behind it and one beside the lamp.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Material</th><th>Passed through</th><th>Came back</th><th>Neither</th></tr>
     <tr><td>the mill's dusty window</td><td>58</td><td>19</td><td>23</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the same window, washed</td><td>90</td><td>7</td><td>3</td></tr>
     <tr><td>fresh milled flour on a tray</td><td>1</td><td>83</td><td>16</td></tr>
     <tr><td>wet wheat bran</td><td>0</td><td>14</td><td>86</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a sheet of baking parchment</td><td>44</td><td>41</td><td>15</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Two ways to report the day's sacks milled to the office at the other end of the mill.<br>Method 1: a pressure gauge on an air line, the needle standing for the count.<br>Method 2: the count punched into a card as holes and read by a machine.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("lamp",74)}${ico("wheat",74)}</div>`,qs:["Compare the dusty window with the washed one. Say where the missing light went in the dusty one and what that costs the miller.","Flour sends back 83 and wet bran only 14, and both are wheat. Explain the difference.","A slow leak lowers the pressure in the air line. Say which method still reports the right number and why, then name what the punched card gives up."],key:["Washing the window took transmission from 58 to 90, a gain of 32 units. The dusty window's extra 12 units of reflection and extra 20 units of absorption account for it: dust scatters some light back out and takes up the rest. The cost to the miller is daylight: a third of the light that would reach the floor never gets in, so lamps have to be lit earlier, which costs money, and the mill floor is harder to inspect for spillage and for a worn belt. Partial: naming the 32 unit gain with nothing said about what the lost daylight costs the miller is approaching. (MS-PS4)","Flour is wheat ground into countless tiny dry grains with air between them, so light meets an enormous number of surfaces and a little reflects at each one, turning most of it back out as a bright white. Wet bran has water filling the gaps, and water's effect on light is much closer to the grain's than air's is, so far less reflects at each boundary and the light goes on into the material and is absorbed, 86 of 100. Same substance, different structure and different filling between the pieces. Partial: saying flour is made of tiny grains, with nothing said about water filling the gaps in the wet bran so less reflects at each boundary, is approaching. (MS-PS4)","The punched card. It is a digital record: a hole is either there or it is not, so nothing about the air line, the weather or the reader's exact setting can turn a 4 into a 5, and the count arrives as sent. The pressure gauge is analog, where the value is the needle's position, so a slow leak drifts the needle and the office writes down a falling count with no sign the mill is still milling the same number of sacks. What the card gives up is speed and fineness: it has to be carried or fed through a reader, and it can only record counts the hole pattern allows. Partial: choosing the card with nothing said about why the gauge hides its own drift, or with nothing named that the card gives up, is approaching. (MS-PS4)"]}]},{id:33,title:"Silt in the Race",short:"Design",parts:[{std:["MS-ETS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Read the situation and the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"defining a design problem with criteria and constraints and evaluating competing designs against them",html:()=>`<p class="big">The mill race silts up every spring and the wheel slows. Clearing it by hand takes four people two days. The mill has $2,000 and one week before the spring flow peaks, and its licence forbids anything that raises the water above the upstream fence line or blocks fish passage. The miller wants no more than 15 percent of the silt left in the race after a spring.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Design</th><th>Cost</th><th>Build time</th><th>Silt left after one spring</th><th>Raises the upstream water</th><th>Blocks fish</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A: a settling pond above the race</td><td>$1,750</td><td>5 days</td><td>8 percent</td><td>no</td><td>no</td></tr>
     <tr><td>B: a weir across the race</td><td>$900</td><td>3 days</td><td>5 percent</td><td>yes, 25 cm above the fence line</td><td>no</td></tr>
     <tr><td>C: a screen with a hand cleared rake</td><td>$350</td><td>1 day</td><td>34 percent</td><td>no</td><td>no</td></tr>
     <tr><td>D: a culvert that bypasses the silt</td><td>$2,400</td><td>6 days</td><td>6 percent</td><td>no</td><td>yes, during the spring run</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("drop",74)}${ico("wave",74)}${ico("ruler",74)}</div>`,qs:["Write out the problem, then sort the chart, and name the figure that is the criterion the mill set for itself.","Work down the chart and say, for each design that does not get through, what stopped it.","Defend the design that is left from the chart, and name the single number you would most want to check before spending the money."],key:["Achieve: keep the mill race clear enough through the spring that the wheel keeps its speed, without hand clearing it every year. Criterion, the measure of success: the percentage of silt left after one spring, with 15 percent as the level the miller set, so that figure is the threshold on the criterion. Constraints, the limits on any solution: no more than $2,000, ready within one week, no raising the water above the upstream fence line, and no blocking fish passage. Partial: a goal stated with the 15 percent filed as a constraint rather than as the threshold on the criterion is approaching. (MS-ETS1)","Design B fails the water level constraint: 25 cm above the fence line is exactly what the licence forbids, and it is the best performer at 5 percent, which is why constraints get checked before performance. Design C fails the criterion: 34 percent left against the 15 percent asked for. Design D fails two constraints: $2,400 against a $2,000 budget, and it blocks fish during the spring run. Only design A fails nothing. Partial: two of the three failing designs judged correctly is approaching, and D's two separate failures are usually counted as one. (MS-ETS1)","Design A: 8 percent silt left clears the 15 percent the miller set, it leaves the water level and the fish passage alone, and at $1,750 and 5 days it fits inside $2,000 and one week, though with little margin on either. Before spending, check the 8 percent figure itself against this race rather than the one it was measured on, because a settling pond only works if the water slows enough inside it to drop its load, and that depends on this stream's spring flow, which the chart never gives. Partial: defending Design A from the chart with no number named that would be checked before spending is approaching. (MS-ETS1)"]},{std:["MS-ETS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the test results. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing test data to find which change helps and planning the next round of testing",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four versions of the settling pond were tried on the same spring flow.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Version</th><th>Pond area</th><th>Pond depth</th><th>Inlet width</th><th>Silt left in the race</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>20 m2</td><td>0.6 m</td><td>1.0 m</td><td>29 percent</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>40 m2</td><td>0.6 m</td><td>1.0 m</td><td>14 percent</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>40 m2</td><td>1.2 m</td><td>1.0 m</td><td>11 percent</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>20 m2</td><td>0.6 m</td><td>0.5 m</td><td>8 percent</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("ruler",74)}${ico("drop",74)}</div>`,qs:["Name the one change between versions 1 and 2 and between 2 and 3, and what each one did.","Version 4 narrowed the inlet instead and left the pond at its original size. Compare its result with versions 2 and 3 and say which change gives the most for the least.","Specify a fifth version, predict its result, and name the variable none of the four runs changed."],key:["Versions 1 to 2: only the pond area changed, 20 m2 to 40 m2, and the silt left fell from 29 percent to 14 percent, a gain of 15 points. Versions 2 to 3: only the depth changed, 0.6 m to 1.2 m, and the silt left fell from 14 percent to 11 percent, a gain of 3 points. One variable each time, so each gain belongs to a cause. Partial: naming both changes with the 15 point and 3 point gains not worked out is approaching. (MS-ETS1)","Version 4 reached 8 percent, the best of the four, with the small 20 m2 pond and only a narrower inlet. Doubling the area cost twice the digging for 15 points, doubling the depth cost twice the depth again for 3 more points, and halving the inlet width cost almost nothing and gained 21 points against version 1. Narrowing the inlet gives much the most for the least: it slows the water entering, and slower water drops its silt, which is what the pond is for. Partial: naming version 4 as the best result with no comparison made of what each change cost is approaching. (MS-ETS1)","Any version argued from the chart. The strongest combines the two changes that worked best: a 0.5 m inlet into a 40 m2 pond at 0.6 m deep, predicting better than 8 percent, while watching for the inlet backing water up toward the fence line, which is a constraint and not just a number. The variable none of the four runs changed is how often the pond is dug out, since a pond that has filled with last year's silt cannot slow this year's water, so the next test should hold the version 4 settings and compare clearing it once a year with clearing it twice. Partial: a fifth version argued from the chart with the dig out interval not identified as the variable never changed is approaching. (MS-ETS1)"]}]}];const LEVELUPS={1:{id:"L1",title:"Level Up: Strong and Thorough",std:["RL.9-10.1"],needs:["RL.8.1"],n:1,dir:"Answer out loud. Read me the exact words for each detail.",html:()=>`<p class="big">Claim: by the end of the story Linnea is an observer of the record rather than a borrower of the notebook. Prove it with strong and thorough evidence. Give at least three details from different parts of the story, and explain what each one shows.</p>${story()}`,key:["Three or more details from across the story, each explained. Examples: she went down “on the fifteenth and on every day after it,” which is the schedule he set rather than a visit. On the twenty-second she applied the rule against what she had actually found and “did not write anything,” which is a judgment only the keeper of a record makes. On the twenty-fourth she “did not have to decide,” which is exactly the state he described, “the day arrives and you know it.” She wrote the date herself, “under forty-one other lines, in the fourth pen,” placing herself in the series. And she brought it to him “because he would want to know,” which is reporting rather than returning. The counterweight, that she “put it in his hands,” should be acknowledged rather than ignored. (HS RL.9-10.1, strong and thorough textual evidence)"]},2:{id:"L2",title:"Level Up: Roots as Exponents",std:["N-RN.2"],needs:["8.EE.A.1"],n:3,dir:"Work on your paper and hold it up. Show the exponent form you used.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Write the square root of 49 as a power with a fractional exponent, and give its value.</li><li>Evaluate 8<sup>2/3</sup>. Show which step you did first.</li><li>Write 5<sup>3/2</sup> in radical form, then give its value to the nearest hundredth.</li></ol>`,key:["49<sup>1/2</sup> = 7. An exponent of 1/2 is the square root, because 49<sup>1/2</sup> × 49<sup>1/2</sup> = 49<sup>1</sup> = 49. (HS N-RN.2)","4. The denominator of the exponent is the root and the numerator is the power, so take the cube root first: 8<sup>1/3</sup> = 2, then square it, 2<sup>2</sup> = 4. Squaring first gives 64 and then the cube root of 64 is also 4, so either order works, but the root first keeps the numbers small. (HS N-RN.2)","The square root of 5<sup>3</sup>, which is the square root of 125, about 11.18. The denominator 2 is the root and the numerator 3 is the power, and 125 is between 121 and 144, so the answer is between 11 and 12. (HS N-RN.2)"]},4:{id:"L4",title:"Level Up: Function Notation",std:["F-IF.1"],needs:["8.F.A.1"],n:3,dir:"Answer out loud. Use the words input, output, domain, and range where they fit.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>The seedling rule is written f(d) = 2.5d + 4, where d is days after planting. Find f(6) and f(0), and say what each one means.</li><li>What domain makes sense for f in this situation, and why is it not every number?</li><li>If f(x) = 5 for some function f, how many different values of x could there be? Use the definition of a function to explain.</li></ol>`,key:["f(6) = 19 and f(0) = 4. f(6) = 2.5(6) + 4 = 19, which is the height in centimetres six days after planting. f(0) = 4, the height at the moment the measuring started. (HS F-IF.1, function notation)","Days from 0 up to the end of the period over which the seedling actually grew at that rate, so whole or part days that are not negative. Negative days are before planting and have no meaning here, and far enough out the model gives a height no seedling reaches, so the domain is the stretch the measurements cover. (HS F-IF.1, domain in context)","Any number of them, including none. A function forbids one input from having two outputs, but it says nothing against two inputs sharing an output, so many x values can give 5. For this particular f, f(x) = 5 gives 2.5x + 4 = 5, so x = 0.4 and there is exactly one. (HS F-IF.1, the definition of a function)"]},6:{id:"L6",title:"Level Up: Same Step or Same Ratio",std:["F-LE.1"],needs:["8.F.A.3"],n:3,dir:"Work on your paper. Tell your teacher each answer and how you decided.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>A tray starts with 180 empty cells and a sower fills 15 of them a minute. Is the number of empty cells a linear function or an exponential function of the minutes? Write the rule.</li><li>Look again at the duckweed table. Linear or exponential? Write a rule for the cover after t days.</li><li>This table shows a seedling's height under a shade cloth. Is it linear or exponential? Write the rule.</li></ol>${bigTable(["Weeks, t","0","1","2","3"],[["Height, v (cm)","80","60","45","33.75"]])}`,key:["Linear. The same amount, 15 cells, is taken away in equal steps, and a linear function is the one with equal differences over equal intervals. The rule is c = 180 − 15t. (HS F-LE.1a and F-LE.1b)","Exponential. The cover is multiplied by the same factor, 2, every two days, rather than having the same amount added, and a function that changes by a constant ratio over equal intervals is exponential. The rule is c = 3 × 2<sup>t/2</sup>, which gives 3, 6, 12, 24 at t = 0, 2, 4, 6. (HS F-LE.1a and F-LE.1c)","Exponential, with the rule v = 80(0.75)<sup>t</sup>. The differences are not constant: 60 − 80 = −20, 45 − 60 = −15, and 33.75 − 45 = −11.25. The ratios are constant: 60 ÷ 80 = 0.75, 45 ÷ 60 = 0.75, and 33.75 ÷ 45 = 0.75. (HS F-LE.1)"]},7:{id:"L7",title:"Level Up: Valid or Only Loud",std:["RI.9-10.8"],needs:["RI.8.8"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Quote the memo when you judge it.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Take the memo's strongest reason and say exactly what it would take to make it a valid argument for the claim, not just a true statement. What is the missing step?</li><li>The Ashby sentence is more than weak evidence: the memo contradicts itself there. Show the contradiction using two quotations, and say what that does to the reader's trust in the rest.</li></ol>${memoText()}`,key:["For the cost reason: $31,000 against $58,000 is true, but on its own it argues only that one plan is cheaper, not that it is the better use of the field. To make it valid, the memo would have to add that the two plans deliver outcomes worth comparing on price, and it never does, because the orchard produces fruit and the meadow holds water, which are not the same good. The yearly figures point the other way over time, $4,200 against the meadow's maintenance, and the memo leaves that out. For the time reason: year four against eight to twelve years is only an argument if sooner is what the field is for, which is the question being argued. Full credit names the missing premise, not just a weakness. (HS RI.9-10.8, assessing whether the reasoning is valid)","Two quotations: “An orchard is productive in year four,” and “Ashby planted a community orchard in 2019, and use of its food pantry dropped the following year, which shows that an orchard feeds people who would otherwise go without.” By the memo's own figure, a 2019 orchard had no crop in 2020, so it cannot be the cause of a 2020 drop. It is also a correlation claimed as a cause with no other explanation ruled out. The effect on the reader is that the memo's own numbers were not checked against each other, which licenses the council to check the 9,000 pounds and the $58,000 rather than take them. (HS RI.9-10.8, identifying fallacious reasoning and false statements)"]},8:{id:"L8",title:"Level Up: Fit a Line to It",std:["S-ID.6"],needs:["8.SP.A.1"],n:3,dir:"Work on your paper with a calculator. Show the two points you used.",mats:["a calculator"],html:()=>`<p class="big">Use the eleven points that lie along the band and ignore the outlier.</p><ol class="qlist"><li>Fit a straight line to the band by picking two points on it, and write its equation.</li><li>What does the slope of your line mean in this situation?</li><li>Use your line to predict the bloom day for a March averaging 46 degrees, then compare it with the point actually plotted at 46 degrees on the band. How far off is the line there?</li></ol>${bloomPlot()}`,key:["Using (41, 30) and (51, 19): the slope is (19 − 30) ÷ (51 − 41) = −11 ÷ 10 = −1.1, and y − 30 = −1.1(x − 41) gives y = −1.1x + 75.1. Any sensible pair of band points that yields a slope near −1.1 and a line through the middle of the band earns the check. (HS S-ID.6a, fitting a function to data)","Each extra degree of average March temperature goes with first bloom arriving about 1.1 days earlier. The sign matters: negative means earlier, not later. (HS S-ID.6c, interpreting the slope)","The line gives −1.1(46) + 75.1 = −50.6 + 75.1 = 24.5, so about April 24 or 25. The plotted band point at 46 degrees is April 24, so the line is about half a day above it, a residual of −0.5 days. That is a close fit. The other point at 46 degrees, April 14, is the outlier and was left out on purpose. (HS S-ID.6b, residuals)"]},9:{id:"L9",title:"Level Up: Phrase It Together",std:["L.9-10.1"],needs:["L.8.1"],n:3,dir:"Say each new sentence out loud, then name the phrase or clause you used.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Combine these into one sentence using a participial phrase: “The notebook lay open on the kitchen table. It showed forty-one Aprils in three pens.”</li><li>Combine these into one sentence using a subordinate clause that begins with Because: “His eyes could no longer judge the day. He left the line blank.”</li><li>Combine these into one sentence using an appositive phrase: “Mr. Okpara handed over the notebook. He is the man who started the record forty-one years ago.”</li></ol>`,key:["“Lying open on the kitchen table, the notebook showed forty-one Aprils in three pens.” Names the participial phrase: Lying open on the kitchen table. Accept any correct version built on a participial phrase. (HS L.9-10.1, various types of phrases and clauses)","“Because his eyes could no longer judge the day, he left the line blank.” Names it as a subordinate clause. (HS L.9-10.1)","“Mr. Okpara, the man who started the record forty-one years ago, handed over the notebook.” Names the appositive phrase: the man who started the record forty-one years ago. (HS L.9-10.1)"]}};const READ_STD=["RL.8.1","RL.8.3","RL.8.4","RI.8.1","RI.8.8","RI.8.9","RL.9-10.1","RI.9-10.8"];const WRITE_STD=["W.8.1","W.8.2","W.8.3","L.9-10.1"];const MATH_STD=["8.NS.A.1","8.EE.A.1","8.EE.A.2","8.EE.B.5","8.EE.C.8","8.F.A.1","8.F.A.3","8.G.A.3","8.G.B.7","8.SP.A.1","N-RN.2","F-IF.1","F-LE.1","S-ID.6"];const TEXT_STD=["RL.8.1","RL.8.3","RL.8.4","RI.8.1","RI.8.8","RI.8.9","W.8.8","RL.9-10.1","RI.9-10.8"];const LOG_NAMES={readq:"Questions read aloud",passage:"Text read aloud by an adult",repeat:"Directions repeated or reworded",vocab:"Word or phrase explained by teacher",hint:"Hint or first step given",fewer:"Fewer questions",time:"Extra time",scribe:"Adult wrote the student’s words",brk:"Break"};function stopLogs(st){const out=["readq"];if(READ_STD.concat(["W.8.8","SL.8.4"]).some(x=>st.std.includes(x)))out.push("passage");out.push("repeat","vocab","hint","fewer","time");if(WRITE_STD.concat(["W.8.8"]).some(x=>st.std.includes(x)))out.push("scribe");out.push("brk");return out}const dl=a=>`<dl>${a.map(x=>`<dt>${x[0]}</dt><dd>${x[1]}</dd>`).join("")}</dl>`;const ul=a=>`<ul class="card-list">${a.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;function wt(st){return st.std.includes("W.8.8")?"notes":st.std.includes("SL.8.4")?"say":st.std.includes("W.8.1")?"argue":st.std.includes("W.8.2")?"explain":"narrative"}function stopSpeak(){try{window.speechSynthesis&&window.speechSynthesis.cancel()}catch(e){}}function guardSpeech(){["mapBtn","teacherBtn","newBtn","prevBtn","gemBtn","skipBtn"].forEach(i=>{const el=document.getElementById(i);if(el&&!el.dataset.tts){el.dataset.tts="1";el.addEventListener("click",stopSpeak)}})}function speakText(t){stopSpeak();guardSpeech();try{const u=new SpeechSynthesisUtterance(t);u.rate=+(document.getElementById("ttsRate")||{value:.9}).value;window.speechSynthesis.speak(u)}catch(e){}}function textOf(sel){return[...document.querySelectorAll(sel)].map(e=>e.textContent.trim()).join(" ")}function listenHtml(){return`<div class="blk-row"><button class="chip" data-say="p">Play the text</button><button class="chip" data-say="q">Play the questions</button><button class="chip" data-say="x">Stop</button></div><label class="small">Speed <select id="ttsRate"><option value="0.75">Slow</option><option value="0.9" selected>Normal</option><option value="1.1">Fast</option></select></label><p class="small">Uses this computer’s voice. If you hear nothing, check the volume, then read the text aloud yourself and mark “Text read aloud by an adult.”</p>`}function listenBind(b){b.querySelectorAll("[data-say]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const k=x.dataset.say,card="#stopView .card ";if(k==="x")return stopSpeak();if(k==="p")speakText(textOf(card+".passage"));if(k==="q")speakText(textOf(card+"ol.qlist > li:not([hidden]), "+card+"> .big"))})}const G_STORY=[["first bloom","the day a tree counts as being in flower, under whatever rule the record uses"],["serviceberry","a small tree that flowers white very early in spring"],["phenology","the study of when recurring natural events happen each year"],["observer","the person who takes a measurement, by the same rule every time"],["record","a run of measurements taken the same way over many years"],["exercise book","a thin school notebook"]];const G_ART=[["phenology","the study of when recurring natural events happen each year"],["temperate","a climate with warm summers and cold winters, not tropical and not polar"],["accumulated","added up over time rather than measured on one day"],["signal","the real pattern a measurement is trying to show"],["scatter","how spread out the measurements are around that pattern"],["pooled","combined with other records so that the pattern shows better"],["drift","change slowly and without anybody noticing"],["data set","a collection of measurements taken the same way"]];const G_FIELD=[["restoration","putting a piece of ground back to a kind of habitat it used to hold"],["wet meadow","a grassland that sits wet for part of the year and holds storm water"],["invasive removal","taking out plants that spread fast and crowd out the rest"],["regrading","reshaping the surface of the ground so that water moves where you want it"],["food pantry","a place that gives out food to people who need it"],["national register","an official list of buildings kept for their history"],["sample","the group of people actually asked in a survey"],["extension office","a county office that gives farmers and gardeners research-based advice"]];const G_WRITE={narrative:[["point of view","who is telling the story"],["pacing","how fast or slow the story moves through its events"],["sensory language","words for what can be seen, heard, felt, smelled, or tasted"],["reflection","a sentence where the narrator says what the events meant"]],notes:[["search terms","the words you type to find sources"],["source","where a fact came from"],["credible","able to be trusted"],["plagiarism","using someone else’s words or ideas as if they were your own"],["paraphrase","to say it in your own words"],["citation","the line that says where a fact came from"]],say:[["recommendation","what you are telling the council to do"],["drawback","the cost or weakness of your own choice"],["at full size","once the trees are mature, not in the first years"]]};function glossHtml(st){if(st.id===14)return dl(G_FIELD);if(st.id===15||st.id===16)return dl(G_ART.concat(G_STORY));if(st.id===13)return dl(G_STORY);if(st.id===11)return dl(G_WRITE.narrative.concat(G_STORY));if(st.id===10)return dl(G_WRITE.notes.concat(G_ART));if(st.id===12)return dl(G_WRITE.say.concat(G_FIELD));if(st.id===5)return dl(G_ART);if(st.id===7)return dl(G_FIELD);return dl(G_STORY)}const TERMS=[["cite","point to the exact words in the text that prove your answer"],["strongest evidence","the words that prove the answer best, not just words on the same topic"],["explicit","stated right in the text"],["inference","something the text does not say outright, but that you can figure out from clues"],["connotation","the feeling a word carries beyond its plain meaning"],["word choice","why this word and not the near one that means almost the same"],["tone","the attitude the writing takes toward its subject"],["claim","what a writer wants you to believe or do"],["relevant","actually about the claim"],["sufficient","enough to prove the point"],["sound reasoning","thinking that makes sense and follows from the evidence"],["irrelevant evidence","a true statement that has nothing to do with the claim"],["thorough evidence","enough details, from more than one place in the text"]];const FRAMES={cite:["The answer is ____. The words that prove it are “____.”","The strongest evidence is “____” because ____.","I can infer that ____ because the text says “____.”"],"RL.8.4":["The word ____ suggests ____, while ____ suggests ____.","The author chose ____ instead of ____, which has the effect of ____.","In plain words the phrase means ____."],"RI.8.8":["Sandoval claims that ____.","One reason she gives is ____, backed by ____.","That evidence is relevant because ____ and sufficient because ____.","The sentence about ____ does not belong because ____.","This reasoning is weak because ____."],"8.SP.A.1":["As ____ goes up, ____ goes ____, so the association is ____.","The points ____ lie close to a straight band, so the association is ____.","The point at ____ is an outlier because ____.","Before taking it out I would check ____."],"RL.8.3":["The line “____” moves the action because ____.","That incident reveals that ____ is ____.","It provokes the decision to ____.","The words “____” are what show it."],"RI.8.9":["Both texts agree that ____.","They disagree on a matter of fact: Text 1 says “____” and Text 2 says “____.”","They disagree on interpretation because Text 1 reads ____ as ____ while Text 2 reads it as ____.","Each gets there because ____."]};function framesHtml(st){const k=st.std.includes("8.SP.A.1")?"8.SP.A.1":st.std.includes("RL.8.3")?"RL.8.3":st.std.includes("RI.8.9")?"RI.8.9":st.std.includes("RI.8.8")||st.std.includes("RI.9-10.8")?"RI.8.8":st.std.includes("RL.8.4")?"RL.8.4":"cite";return`<ul class="starters" style="font-size:1.2rem">${FRAMES[k].map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`}function gramHtml(){return`<h4>Verbals: verb forms doing another job</h4>`+dl([["Gerund","ends in -ing and acts as a noun. <em>Keeping the record takes time.</em>"],["Participle","often ends in -ing or -ed and describes a noun. <em>The held-together notebook sat on the table.</em>"],["Infinitive","to plus a verb. <em>Her job was to stand at the gate.</em>"]])+`<h4>Voice</h4>`+dl([["Active","the subject does the action. <em>Mr. Okpara handed over the notebook.</em>"],["Passive","the subject receives the action. <em>The notebook was handed over by Mr. Okpara.</em>"]])+`<h4>Mood</h4>`+dl([["Indicative","states a fact. <em>The tree is in bloom.</em>"],["Imperative","gives a direction. <em>Write the date the evening you see it.</em>"],["Interrogative","asks a question. <em>Is the tree in bloom?</em>"],["Conditional","tells what would or could happen. <em>I would go if the gate were open.</em>"],["Subjunctive","a wish or something not true. Uses were. <em>I wish the tree were easier to see.</em>"]])+`<h4>Phrases and clauses</h4>`+dl([["Participial phrase","a participle plus its words, describing a noun. <em>Lying open on the table, the notebook showed three pens.</em>"],["Subordinate clause","has a subject and a verb but cannot stand alone. <em>Because his eyes had changed, he left the line blank.</em>"],["Appositive phrase","renames the noun right beside it. <em>Linnea, the fourth observer, wrote the date.</em>"]])+`<p class="small">Keep the same voice and the same mood all the way through a sentence.</p>`}const PLAN={argue:["My claim","What the other choice has going for it","Reason 1 and the evidence for it","Reason 2 and the evidence for it","My conclusion"],explain:["What the letter is about","What the record is","How it was taken","What a user needs to watch for","How I close it"],narrative:["Who, where, and when","The rule I was given","What happened first, next, last","A line of dialogue I will use","How it ended and what it meant"],notes:["Which source I trust, and why","My search terms","My quote or paraphrase","My citation"],say:["My recommendation","My strongest evidence from the table","The drawback","What I need from the council"]};function planHtml(st){const S=E.state;S.plan=S.plan||{};const k=wt(st),v=S.plan[k]||[];return`<div class="planner">${PLAN[k].map((x,i)=>`<label>${x}<textarea data-pi="${i}">${(v[i]||"").replace(/</g,"&lt;")}</textarea></label>`).join("")}</div><p class="small">Your plan is saved for the speaking stop and the writing stop.</p>`}function planBind(b,r,st){b.querySelectorAll("[data-pi]").forEach(t=>t.oninput=()=>{const S=E.state,k=wt(st);S.plan=S.plan||{};S.plan[k]=S.plan[k]||[];S.plan[k][+t.dataset.pi]=t.value;E.save()})}const SENT={narrative:{s:["It was ____ when ____.","At first, ____.","“____,” ____ said.","A few minutes later, ____.","By the time ____, ____.","Looking back, I understood that ____."],w:["at first","a few minutes later","suddenly","after that","by the time","finally","looking back"]},notes:{s:["I trust Source ____ more because ____.","These search terms are best because ____.","Dr. Yusuf Adeyemi reports, “____.”","According to Dr. Yusuf Adeyemi, ____.","Author. “Title.” Publisher, year."],w:["according to","reports","states","explains"]},say:{s:["The council should fund ____.","My strongest evidence is ____.","The drawback is ____.","For that reason, ____.","From the council I need ____."],w:["because","the table shows","compared with","by contrast","for that reason"]},argue:{s:["The town should ____.","It is true that ____. Even so, ____.","One reason is ____, because the article says “____.”","A second reason is ____.","For these reasons, ____."],w:["because","even so","however","in addition","which means that","for these reasons"]},explain:{s:["This letter describes ____.","The record is ____.","It was taken by ____, using the rule that ____.","Anyone using the data should know that ____.","In short, ____."],w:["first","in addition","however","as a result","in short"]}};function sentHtml(st){const d=SENT[wt(st)]||SENT.narrative;return`<ul class="starters">${d.s.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul><h4>Linking words</h4><div class="wordbank">${d.w.map(x=>`<span>${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`}const CHECK={narrative:["I set the scene.","Events are in an order that makes sense.","I used at least two lines of dialogue.","I used details the reader can see, hear, or feel.","I used time words.","My ending reflects on what happened.","I reread it for mistakes."],argue:["My claim is in the first sentence.","I said what the other choice has going for it.","Every reason has evidence behind it.","My evidence is quoted or stated accurately.","I used linking words between claim, reason and evidence.","The style is formal, with no slang.","My last sentence follows from the argument."],explain:["It is laid out as a letter, with a date, an addressee, a greeting and a sign-off.","My first sentence says what the letter is about.","The information is grouped into parts.","I used facts and details, not general statements.","I explained any term the reader may not know.","The style is formal.","My last sentence follows from the information."]};function checkHtml(st){const k=CHECK[wt(st||{std:[]})]?wt(st||{std:[]}):"narrative";return`<ul class="card-list" style="list-style:none;padding-left:0">${CHECK[k].map(x=>`<li><label style="display:flex;gap:8px;align-items:flex-start"><input type="checkbox" style="width:20px;height:20px;margin-top:4px"> ${x}</label></li>`).join("")}</ul>`}let calcE="",calcAns="";function calcEval(){let e=calcE.replace(/×/g,"*").replace(/÷/g,"/").replace(/−/g,"-").replace(/\^/g,"**").replace(/√\(/g,"Math.sqrt(");if(!/^[0-9+\-*/().\sMathsqr]*$/.test(e)||!e)return"";try{const o=(e.match(/\(/g)||[]).length-(e.match(/\)/g)||[]).length;if(o>0)e+=")".repeat(o);const v=Function('"use strict";return ('+e+")")();return Number.isFinite(v)?String(+v.toPrecision(10)):"Error"}catch(x){return"Error"}}function calcHtml(){const keys=["7","8","9","÷","⌫","4","5","6","×","(","1","2","3","−",")","0",".","^","+","C","√(","x²","="];return`<div class="calc-disp" aria-live="polite">${calcE||"0"}${calcAns!==""?`<br><strong>= ${calcAns}</strong>`:""}</div><div class="calc-keys">${keys.map(k=>`<button data-k="${k}" class="${k==="="?"eq":/[0-9.]/.test(k)?"":"op"}">${k==="^"?"xʸ":k==="√("?"√":k}</button>`).join("")}</div><p class="small">Press √ then the number and close the bracket. For a square, press x². A negative exponent has to be typed in brackets, such as 2^(-3).</p>`}function calcBind(b,r){b.querySelectorAll("[data-k]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const k=x.dataset.k;if(k==="C"){calcE="";calcAns=""}else if(k==="⌫"){calcE=calcE.replace(/(√\(|.)$/,"");calcAns=""}else if(k==="="){calcAns=calcEval()}else{if(calcAns!==""&&calcAns!=="Error"&&/^[+−×÷^x]/.test(k))calcE=calcAns;else if(calcAns!=="")calcE="";calcAns="";calcE+=k==="x²"?"^2":k}r()})}const MULT_N=12;let multSel=[0,0];function multHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>×</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++)h+=`<th data-r="0" data-c="${c}" class="${multSel[1]===c?"hl":""}">${c}</th>`;h+="</tr>";for(let r=1;r<=MULT_N;r++){h+=`<tr><th data-r="${r}" data-c="0" class="${multSel[0]===r?"hl":""}">${r}</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++){const hr=multSel[0]===r,hc=multSel[1]===c;h+=`<td data-r="${r}" data-c="${c}" class="${hr&&hc?"pick":hr||hc?"hl":""}">${r*c}</td>`}h+="</tr>"}return h+`</table></div><p class="small">Click a number to light up its row and column. Click it again to clear.</p>`}function powHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>n</th><th>n<sup>2</sup></th><th>n<sup>3</sup></th><th>2<sup>n</sup></th><th>3<sup>n</sup></th></tr>`;for(let n=1;n<=10;n++)h+=`<tr><th>${n}</th><td>${n*n}</td><td>${n*n*n}</td><td>${Math.pow(2,n)}</td><td>${Math.pow(3,n)}</td></tr>`;return h+`</table></div><p class="small">Anything to the power 0 is 1. A negative exponent is the reciprocal of the positive one, so 2<sup>−3</sup> = 1 ÷ 8.</p>`}const rules=rows=>`<table class="meas">${rows.map(r=>`<tr><th>${r[0]}</th></tr><tr><td class="formula">${r[1]}</td></tr>`).join("")}</table>`;const FORM={"8.EE.A.1":["Properties of integer exponents",rules([["Multiply","a<sup>m</sup> × a<sup>n</sup> = a<sup>m+n</sup>"],["Divide","a<sup>m</sup> ÷ a<sup>n</sup> = a<sup>m−n</sup>"],["Power of a power","(a<sup>m</sup>)<sup>n</sup> = a<sup>mn</sup>"],["Zero","a<sup>0</sup> = 1"],["Negative","a<sup>−n</sup> = 1 ÷ a<sup>n</sup>"]])+ul(["These only combine exponents when the base is the same.","A negative exponent never makes the answer negative. It moves the power to the bottom of a fraction."])],"8.F.A.1":["Functions",ul(["A function is a rule that gives each input exactly one output.","Two different inputs may share an output. One input may not have two outputs.","The graph of a function is the set of ordered pairs (input, output).","Vertical line test: a vertical line touches the graph of a function at most once."])],"8.F.A.3":["Linear functions",`<p class="formula">y = mx + b</p>`+ul(["m is the slope, the change in y for each 1 of x. b is the value when x is 0.","The graph is a straight line, and the change is the same over every equal step.","If the rule squares x, or puts x on the bottom of a fraction, or uses x as an exponent, it is not linear.","To test a table: equal steps in x should give equal changes in y."])],"8.SP.A.1":["Reading a scatter plot",ul(["<strong>Positive association:</strong> as one measurement rises the other rises too, so the cloud slopes up.","<strong>Negative association:</strong> as one rises the other falls, so the cloud slopes down.","<strong>Linear:</strong> the points lie close to a straight band. <strong>Nonlinear:</strong> the band bends.","<strong>No association:</strong> the points sit in a cloud with no slope either way.","<strong>Clustering:</strong> the points gather into separate groups.","<strong>Outlier:</strong> one point well away from the pattern. Check it before you remove it."])],"F-LE.1":["Linear or exponential",ul(["<strong>Linear:</strong> equal differences over equal intervals. The same amount is added or taken away each step.","<strong>Exponential:</strong> equal ratios over equal intervals. The value is multiplied by the same factor each step.","To test a table: find the differences first, then the ratios. Whichever is constant names the kind."])],"N-RN.2":["Rational exponents",rules([["Root as a power","a<sup>1/n</sup> is the nth root of a"],["Both at once","a<sup>m/n</sup> is the nth root of a, raised to the m"]])+ul(["The bottom of the exponent is the root. The top is the power.","Take the root first when you can. The numbers stay smaller."])],"S-ID.6":["Fitting a line to data",ul(["Pick two points on the band, not two actual data points unless they sit on it.","Slope = the change in y divided by the change in x, then use y − y<sub>1</sub> = m(x − x<sub>1</sub>).","A residual is the real value minus the value the line predicts.","A line fitted inside the data can be used to estimate. Outside the data it is a guess."])],"8.NS.A.1":["Rational and irrational numbers",ul(["A rational number can be written as a fraction of two whole numbers. Its decimal expansion either terminates or settles into a repeating block.","An irrational number cannot. Its expansion never terminates and never repeats. The square root of 2 and pi are the usual examples.","To turn a repeating decimal into a fraction: multiply by 10 to the power of the length of the repeat, subtract the original, and solve.","Going on forever does not make a number irrational. 1/3 goes on forever and is rational."])],"8.EE.A.2":["Square roots and cube roots",rules([["x² = p","x = ±√p, both roots"],["x³ = p","x = ∛p, one root"]])+ul(["Evaluate small perfect squares and cubes from memory: 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81, 100 and 1, 8, 27, 64, 125.","√2 is irrational: no fraction of whole numbers squares to 2.","A square root equation has two solutions. A cube root equation has one."])],"8.EE.B.5":["Proportional graphs and slope",`<p class="formula">y = mx</p>`+ul(["A proportional relationship graphs as a straight line through the origin.","The unit rate IS the slope: the rise in y for each 1 of x.","To compare a graph with an equation, pull the unit rate out of each and compare those two numbers.","The steeper line is the faster rate."])],"8.EE.C.8":["Pairs of linear equations",ul(["A solution is a pair (x, y) that satisfies both equations, which is where the two lines cross.","Substitution: get one letter alone, then put that expression into the other equation.","Elimination: add or subtract the equations so that one letter cancels.","Same slope and different intercepts: parallel lines, no solution. Same line twice: infinitely many."])],"8.G.A.3":["Transformations in coordinates",rules([["Translate a right, b up","(x, y) → (x + a, y + b)"],["Reflect across the x-axis","(x, y) → (x, −y)"],["Reflect across the y-axis","(x, y) → (−x, y)"],["Rotate 90° counterclockwise about the origin","(x, y) → (−y, x)"],["Rotate 180° about the origin","(x, y) → (−x, −y)"],["Dilate from the origin by k","(x, y) → (kx, ky)"]])+ul(["Translations, reflections and rotations keep both side lengths and angles.","A dilation multiplies every length by k and leaves every angle alone."])],"8.G.B.7":["The Pythagorean theorem",`<p class="formula">a² + b² = c²</p>`+ul(["c is the hypotenuse, the side opposite the right angle, and it is always the longest.","In three dimensions, do it twice: find the diagonal of the base, then use that as a leg with the height.","Leave the answer as a square root if it is not exact, then round, and keep the units."])],"F-IF.1":["Function notation",ul(["f(6) means the output when the input is 6. It is not f times 6.","The domain is the set of inputs the function is allowed to take.","The range is the set of outputs it produces.","In a real situation the domain is usually narrower than every number."])]};function formHtml(st){const seen=[];return st.std.filter(x=>FORM[x]).map(x=>{if(seen.includes(FORM[x][0]))return"";seen.push(FORM[x][0]);return`<h4>${FORM[x][0]}</h4>${FORM[x][1]}`}).join("")}const EX={"8.EE.A.1":[["Combine powers of one base",["Simplify (2<sup>6</sup> × 2<sup>−4</sup>) ÷ 2<sup>3</sup>.","Multiplying adds: 6 + (−4) = 2, so the top is 2<sup>2</sup>.","Dividing subtracts: 2 − 3 = −1.","The answer is 2<sup>−1</sup>, which is 1/2."]]],"8.F.A.3":[["Decide whether a table is linear",["Table: x = 0, 1, 2, 3 and y = 5, 9, 13, 17.","x goes up by 1 each step, so check the changes in y: 4, 4, 4.","The changes are equal, so it is linear.","b is the value at x = 0, which is 5, and m is the change, which is 4, so y = 4x + 5."]]],"8.SP.A.1":[["Describe a scatter plot",["Look along the bottom axis first and say what is being measured there.","Ask whether the cloud slopes up, slopes down, or neither. That gives positive, negative, or none.","Ask whether it is straight or bent. That gives linear or nonlinear.","Then look for a point far from the rest, and for gaps that split the points into groups."]]],"S-ID.6":[["Fit a line and find a residual",["Points lie near a band from (2, 14) down to (10, 6).","Slope = (6 − 14) ÷ (10 − 2) = −8 ÷ 8 = −1.","Through (2, 14): y − 14 = −1(x − 2), so y = −x + 16.","At x = 5 the line gives 11. If the real point is (5, 10), the residual is 10 − 11 = −1."]]]};function exHtml(st){const seen=[];return st.std.filter(x=>EX[x]).map(x=>EX[x].map(e=>{if(seen.includes(e[0]))return"";seen.push(e[0]);return`<h4>${e[0]}</h4><ol class="card-list">${e[1].map(s=>`<li>${s}</li>`).join("")}</ol>`}).join("")).join("")+`<p class="small">These are different problems from the ones on the stop. Use the same steps.</p>`}let gridPts=[],gridJoin=false;const GS=440,GM=10,GU=(GS-68)/(2*GM);function pgridHtml(){const lines=[];if(gridJoin)for(let i=1;i<gridPts.length;i++)lines.push([gridPts[i-1],gridPts[i]]);return`<div class="blk-row"><button class="chip" data-g="join" aria-pressed="${gridJoin}">Connect the points</button><button class="chip" data-g="undo">Undo</button><button class="chip" data-g="clear">Clear</button></div><div class="figure" data-pg style="cursor:crosshair">${F.coordGrid({max:GM,quad4:true,size:GS,points:gridPts,lines})}</div><p class="small">Click the grid to plot a point. Points: ${gridPts.map(p=>`(${p[0]}, ${p[1]})`).join(", ")||"none yet"}</p>`}function pgridBind(b,r){b.querySelectorAll("[data-g]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const g=x.dataset.g;if(g==="join")gridJoin=!gridJoin;if(g==="undo")gridPts.pop();if(g==="clear")gridPts=[];r()});const el=b.querySelector("[data-pg] svg");if(!el)return;el.onclick=e=>{const q=el.getBoundingClientRect(),vx=(e.clientX-q.left)*GS/q.width,vy=(e.clientY-q.top)*GS/q.height,x=Math.round((vx-34)/GU)-GM,y=Math.round((GS-34-vy)/GU)-GM;if(Math.abs(x)>GM||Math.abs(y)>GM)return;gridPts.push([x,y]);r()}}const TOOLS={listen:{name:"Computer reads it aloud",std:TEXT_STD,ok:()=>"speechSynthesis"in window,html:listenHtml,bind:listenBind},gloss:{name:"Word help",std:TEXT_STD.concat(WRITE_STD,["SL.8.4"]),html:glossHtml},terms:{name:"Reading terms",std:READ_STD,html:()=>dl(TERMS)},frames:{name:"Answer frames",std:READ_STD.concat(["8.SP.A.1"]),html:framesHtml},gram:{name:"Grammar card",std:["L.8.1","L.9-10.1"],html:gramHtml},plan:{name:"Planner",std:WRITE_STD.concat(["W.8.8","SL.8.4"]),html:planHtml,bind:planBind},sent:{name:"Sentence frames",std:WRITE_STD.concat(["W.8.8","SL.8.4"]),html:sentHtml},check:{name:"Writing checklist",std:WRITE_STD,html:checkHtml},calc:{name:"Calculator",std:MATH_STD,html:calcHtml,bind:calcBind},mult:{name:"Multiplication chart",std:MATH_STD,html:multHtml,bind:(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll("[data-r]").forEach(c=>c.onclick=()=>{const x=+c.dataset.r,k=+c.dataset.c;multSel=multSel[0]===x&&multSel[1]===k?[0,0]:[x,k];r()})},pow:{name:"Powers chart",std:["8.EE.A.1","8.EE.A.2","8.NS.A.1","8.G.B.7","N-RN.2"],html:powHtml},form:{name:"Formula and definition card",std:Object.keys(FORM),html:formHtml},ex:{name:"Worked example",std:Object.keys(EX),html:exHtml},grid:{name:"Coordinate grid",std:["8.F.A.1","8.F.A.3","8.EE.B.5","8.G.A.3","8.SP.A.1","F-IF.1","F-LE.1","S-ID.6"],html:pgridHtml,bind:pgridBind}};const SUP_CAVEATS=[{t:"listen",std:READ_STD,msg:"If the computer read the text aloud, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading."},{t:"passage",msg:"If the text or the table was read to the student, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading."},{t:"terms",std:READ_STD,msg:"The reading terms card defines cite, inference, connotation, word choice, relevant, sufficient, and irrelevant evidence, so this stop then shows applying those ideas with a reference."},{t:"frames",msg:"Answer frames give the sentence structure. Rate the evidence and the thinking the student supplied."},{t:"frames",std:["8.SP.A.1"],msg:"The frames for this stop name positive, negative, linear, and outlier, so count the naming part of each answer as supported and rate the reading of the plot."},{t:"gram",std:["L.8.1","L.9-10.1"],msg:"The grammar card defines verbals, voice, mood, and phrase types with examples, so this stop shows using a reference, not recall."},{t:"plan",msg:"The planner supplies the organization, so count the organization check as supported, not independent."},{t:"sent",msg:"Sentence frames supply the linking words and the closing, so count those checks as supported."},{t:"check",msg:"The checklist names what to include. Note that the student used it to revise."},{t:"calc",std:["8.EE.A.1","N-RN.2"],msg:"The calculator has a power key, so rate whether the student combined the exponents correctly rather than whether the final number is right."},{t:"calc",std:["8.F.A.1","8.F.A.3","8.SP.A.1","F-LE.1","S-ID.6","F-IF.1"],msg:"The calculator worked out the values. Rate whether the student knew what to compute and what the result shows."},{t:"pow",std:["8.EE.A.1","N-RN.2"],msg:"The powers chart gives the squares, cubes, and powers of 2 and 3, so count the final evaluation of each answer as supported and rate the exponent rule the student used."},{t:"form",std:["8.EE.A.1","N-RN.2"],msg:"The card lists every exponent rule this stop asks about, so the stop then shows applying a stated rule rather than recalling it."},{t:"form",std:["8.F.A.1","F-IF.1"],msg:"The card states the definition of a function and the vertical line test, so this stop then shows applying a stated rule rather than recalling it."},{t:"form",std:["8.F.A.3","F-LE.1"],msg:"The card says how to test a table and which forms are not linear, so count the sorting questions as supported."},{t:"form",std:["8.SP.A.1","S-ID.6"],msg:"The card names every pattern this stop asks for and gives the line-fitting steps, so count the naming and the method as supported and rate what the student read off the plot."},{t:"ex",msg:"A worked example models the method. Count the matching questions as supported."},{t:"grid",std:["8.F.A.1","8.F.A.3","S-ID.6"],msg:"If the grid tool was used to plot the points or the line, note that the student worked from a picture rather than from the rule."},{t:"scribe",msg:"If an adult wrote, rate the ideas and the organization. Do not rate conventions the adult supplied."},{t:"fewer",msg:"If questions were skipped, change the “of” number in the score to the number the student tried."},{t:"hint",msg:"Note which question got the hint, and count that question as supported."},{t:"form",std:["8.NS.A.1"],msg:"The card gives the rational and irrational test and the whole method for turning a repeating decimal into a fraction, so count those answers as supported, not independent."},{t:"form",std:["8.EE.A.2"],msg:"The card lists the perfect squares and cubes and states that x squared equals p has two roots, so count the evaluation as supported and rate the reasoning about the square root of 2."},{t:"form",std:["8.EE.B.5"],msg:"The card says outright that the unit rate is the slope, which is most of the first question. Rate the comparison between the graph and the equation."},{t:"form",std:["8.EE.C.8"],msg:"The card gives both solving methods and the parallel-lines case, so count the method and the no-solution answer as supported."},{t:"form",std:["8.G.A.3"],msg:"The card gives the coordinate rule for every transformation on this stop, so the stop then shows applying a stated rule rather than recalling it. Write that on the record sheet."},{t:"form",std:["8.G.B.7"],msg:"The card gives the theorem and the two-step method for three dimensions, so count the setup as supported and rate the arithmetic and the units."},{t:"pow",std:["8.EE.A.2","8.NS.A.1","8.G.B.7"],msg:"The powers chart gives the squares and cubes, so the roots can be read straight off it. Note it."},{t:"calc",std:["8.NS.A.1","8.EE.A.2","8.EE.B.5","8.EE.C.8","8.G.A.3","8.G.B.7"],msg:"The calculator has a root key and a power key. Rate whether the student set each one up correctly and kept the units, not the computation."},{t:"grid",std:["8.EE.B.5","8.G.A.3"],msg:"If the grid tool was used to plot the image or the second line, note that the student worked from a picture rather than from the coordinate rule."},{t:"frames",std:["RL.8.3","RI.8.9"],msg:"The frames name what each answer has to contain, including the fact-versus-interpretation split, so count the shape of the answer as supported and rate the evidence the student chose."}];return{stops:STOPS,levelups:LEVELUPS,tools:TOOLS,supCaveats:SUP_CAVEATS,logNames:LOG_NAMES,stopLogs,onToolChange:()=>stopSpeak()}}});
